WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.993
Hey, everybody.

2
00:00:00.993 --> 00:00:01.830
Welcome to the podcast.

3
00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.949
It's time to turn on and tune in

4
00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:08.750
because today's guest is the OG of integrative medicine.

5
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.298
A true pioneer of health, a legend of mind-body healing

6
00:00:12.298 --> 00:00:17.030
the medical mystic himself, Dr. Andrew Weil.

7
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.260
A graduate of Harvard and Harvard Medical School.

8
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.909
Dr. Weil has spent basically the last 50 plus years

9
00:00:21.909 --> 00:00:25.451
studying and evangelizing holistic wellness,

10
00:00:25.451 --> 00:00:28.896
underappreciated healing modalities, medicinal plants

11
00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:32.810
and the reform of medical education and practice.

12
00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.520
Named one of the 100 most influential people in the world

13
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.353
by time magazine.

14
00:00:36.353 --> 00:00:39.813
He's also a New York times best-selling author of 15 books.

15
00:00:39.813 --> 00:00:43.770
He's the founder and director of the Andrew Weil Center

16
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.490
for Integrative Medicine at the University of Arizona.

17
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.951
As well as the founder of Matcha tea brand, Matcha Kari

18
00:00:49.951 --> 00:00:52.360
and a partner in True Kitchen.

19
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.683
A chain of something like 35 healthy food restaurants.

20
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:57.270
This man is a gift

21
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.924
and this is a wonderful wide-ranging conversation

22
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:03.179
that spans his extraordinary life

23
00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:05.479
and delves into many important aspects

24
00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:08.050
of human and planetary health.

25
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.720
So please hit that subscribe button,

26
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.942
click that notification bell thing and enjoy.

27
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.770
It's a pleasure to meet you.

28
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:23.603
It's an honor.

29
00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:24.563
Thanks for taking the time.

30
00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:26.989
I'm excited to be here with you.

31
00:01:26.989 --> 00:01:30.823
I don't even know where to begin this other than to say

32
00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:33.679
you've been in this game for a long time.

33
00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:38.170 line:15%
You're a pioneer and icon in the integrative medicine space.

34
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.910 line:15%
And so much of what you're about.

35
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.700
You were so far ahead of the curve.

36
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.770
Like culture is only now even just beginning to catch up

37
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:49.510
to so many other things you were talking about

38
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.840
in the late sixties, throughout the seventies,

39
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.430
when you were kind of like this iconic class

40
00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.433
I guess in some cases you still maybe considered that.

41
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:00.266
I don't know, I hope so.
(words lost in cross talk)

42
00:02:00.266 --> 00:02:01.267
You're welcomed.

43
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.610
(laughs)

44
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.028
Now in a way that it probably didn't feel that way

45
00:02:05.028 --> 00:02:07.340
in the earlier stages of your career.

46
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.145
Well if I seize being controversial

47
00:02:09.145 --> 00:02:10.855
I think I'm not doing my job.

48
00:02:10.855 --> 00:02:11.700
(laughs)
(indistinct)

49
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.612
There is a little bit of a I'm interested in that spirit.

50
00:02:14.612 --> 00:02:17.598
There's a little bit of a punk rock thing going on here.

51
00:02:17.598 --> 00:02:21.218
'Cause you've been pushing the envelope right from day one

52
00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:24.117
from what you were writing, in the Crimson

53
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:26.829
at Harvard and all the kind of stuff that dates back

54
00:02:26.829 --> 00:02:30.370
to you being in your like late teens and early twenties.

55
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.217
Definitely in the first book that I wrote in 1972

56
00:02:33.217 --> 00:02:34.547
"The Natural Mind".

57
00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.087
I went back and read some parts of it

58
00:02:38.087 --> 00:02:40.030
and it really predicts what's happening now

59
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.783
with psychedelics and the positive change

60
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:44.831
that this can bring to our society.

61
00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:48.000
But for a long time, I've written

62
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.283
and said the same thing really for almost 50 years.

63
00:02:51.283 --> 00:02:53.946
And for the early part of that time

64
00:02:53.946 --> 00:02:56.050
nobody paid attention to me.

65
00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.070
And then I got a larger and larger following

66
00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.525
in the general public but none of my medical colleagues

67
00:03:00.525 --> 00:03:02.390
paid any attention to me.

68
00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.057
And then that changed in the early 1990s.

69
00:03:05.057 --> 00:03:08.280
And now integrative medicine is becoming mainstream

70
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.888
and it's very gratifying to watch.

71
00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:10.827
Yeah it's gotta be,

72
00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:13.312
it's gotta feel like you've been vindicated.

73
00:03:13.312 --> 00:03:14.960
I mean, there's still so much work to be done

74
00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.350
So much but it feels like the culture has caught up

75
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.073
with me in a lot of respects.

76
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.515
So it must be challenging to check your ego

77
00:03:22.515 --> 00:03:23.568
a little bit with that.

78
00:03:23.568 --> 00:03:24.401
(laughs)

79
00:03:24.401 --> 00:03:26.919
You're like screaming about this stuff forever.

80
00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:28.827
Well, it seems so obvious to me

81
00:03:28.827 --> 00:03:30.382
the things that I've been advocating

82
00:03:30.382 --> 00:03:34.318
talking about, one of my main principles

83
00:03:34.318 --> 00:03:36.530
is that the body can heal itself.

84
00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:37.890
That's hardly news.

85
00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.750
I mean, that's Hippocrates said that

86
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:40.583
in the fifth century BC.

87
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:43.210
It's just that it hasn't been much paid attention to

88
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.130
in modern medicine.

89
00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.248
Yeah, well, I feel like so much of your perspective

90
00:03:47.248 --> 00:03:50.736
was well-informed by all the traveling that you did

91
00:03:50.736 --> 00:03:51.847
as a young person.

92
00:03:51.847 --> 00:03:55.335
Seeing parts of the world that were relatively inaccessible

93
00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:58.059
at the time to give you a broader perspective

94
00:03:58.059 --> 00:04:01.243
on different modalities of health and healing.

95
00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.791
I had a wonderful opportunity that came my way by chance.

96
00:04:06.791 --> 00:04:11.791
When I was 17, I won a scholarship to an experimental school

97
00:04:13.155 --> 00:04:17.160
that took a group of 22 students and six faculty people

98
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.929
around the world for a year.

99
00:04:18.929 --> 00:04:21.060
And we lived with native families.

100
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.954
So I did this a year between high school and college

101
00:04:22.954 --> 00:04:24.219
that changed my life.

102
00:04:24.219 --> 00:04:28.887
And I think traveling has been so important to me

103
00:04:28.887 --> 00:04:31.119
and to be in other cultures

104
00:04:31.119 --> 00:04:32.685
and to see that there are other ways

105
00:04:32.685 --> 00:04:36.391
of interpreting reality, incredibly valuable.

106
00:04:36.391 --> 00:04:39.275
I think that's one of the problems I see in our society.

107
00:04:39.275 --> 00:04:41.675
We're a big isolated country

108
00:04:41.675 --> 00:04:44.944
and many people have no awareness of what's out there.

109
00:04:44.944 --> 00:04:48.755
I saw some statistic a few years ago that I think it

110
00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:52.588
was fewer than 40% of members of Congress had passports.

111
00:04:52.588 --> 00:04:54.173
I mean, amazing.

112
00:04:54.173 --> 00:04:55.006
Really?

113
00:04:55.006 --> 00:04:57.707
Yeah. And George W. Bush had never been out

114
00:04:57.707 --> 00:04:59.857
of the country when he became president.

115
00:04:59.857 --> 00:05:01.380
I mean, how is that possible?

116
00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:02.716
He was the son of a president

117
00:05:02.716 --> 00:05:04.628
and he'd never been out of the country.

118
00:05:04.628 --> 00:05:06.350
It does seem impossible.

119
00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.146
Amazing, but I think there's no substitute

120
00:05:09.146 --> 00:05:12.913
for getting perspectives of other societies and cultures.

121
00:05:12.913 --> 00:05:15.747
I mean, the most interesting and introspective people

122
00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:17.738
that I know are people that are well-traveled.

123
00:05:17.738 --> 00:05:20.681
And particularly people that took the opportunity

124
00:05:20.681 --> 00:05:23.300
to travel when they were young and they're kind of,

125
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.636
their minds were still being formed

126
00:05:25.636 --> 00:05:29.260
and they weren't calcified around their world views.

127
00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.962
And then after I finished my medical training,

128
00:05:31.962 --> 00:05:33.500
I then for a number of years,

129
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.944
I traveled a lot in Latin America.

130
00:05:35.944 --> 00:05:38.121
I lived in Latin America for a number of years

131
00:05:38.121 --> 00:05:41.038
studying plants and drugs and other ways of healing

132
00:05:41.038 --> 00:05:43.664
and then also in Africa and Asia.

133
00:05:43.664 --> 00:05:48.548
And so that's just been very much a part of my life.

134
00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:51.840
Well, I'm obsessed with Harvard in the mid sixties.

135
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.108
I just can't get enough of learning about

136
00:05:55.108 --> 00:05:59.827
what an interesting time it was in Cambridge with

137
00:05:59.827 --> 00:06:03.809
everybody from Richard Alpert to Timothy Leary of course,

138
00:06:03.809 --> 00:06:07.692
Skinner who I know you spend time with him.

139
00:06:07.692 --> 00:06:10.436
Was there a sense that it was special then?

140
00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:13.048
Well, Harvard has always had a sense of being special.

141
00:06:13.048 --> 00:06:16.298
I remember there was a novel written by a faculty wife

142
00:06:16.298 --> 00:06:18.717
at Harvard called, "We Happy Few".

143
00:06:18.717 --> 00:06:22.490
So there was always the sense that we were this elite.

144
00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.201
But frankly Harvard was getting passed by

145
00:06:25.201 --> 00:06:27.860
when the sixties revolution started.

146
00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.460
That was all going on in Berkeley and the West Coast.

147
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.716
And it was a few years before it got to Harvard

148
00:06:34.716 --> 00:06:37.703
and Harvard started to catch up with what was happening.

149
00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.780
Did you have access to Leary and Alpert?

150
00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.913
I did, I had become interested in psychedelics.

151
00:06:45.913 --> 00:06:48.580
I'd read about them right before I entered Harvard

152
00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.883
as a freshman in 1960.

153
00:06:51.804 --> 00:06:56.804
And I asked about how I could find out about these.

154
00:06:57.528 --> 00:07:01.549
And I had a psychology professor who told me

155
00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:04.291
that there was these two guys

156
00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:06.090
in the social relations department

157
00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:07.991
who were starting to study these drugs.

158
00:07:07.991 --> 00:07:10.765
So I went over and met Leary at his office

159
00:07:10.765 --> 00:07:12.027
and had a conversation.

160
00:07:12.027 --> 00:07:14.582
This is from about 1960, 61.

161
00:07:14.582 --> 00:07:17.576
Before he really we was just getting going on studies

162
00:07:17.576 --> 00:07:20.432
he was doing, he was fascinating.

163
00:07:20.432 --> 00:07:22.401
He had a twinkle in his eye.

164
00:07:22.401 --> 00:07:27.401
He was a kind of Irish storyteller,

165
00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:33.210
very charming and he genuinely thought that these drugs

166
00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.560
these were the most interesting things

167
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.650
he'd ever come across.

168
00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:36.734
And he thought they had the potential

169
00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:37.911
to completely change society.

170
00:07:37.911 --> 00:07:41.074
And he had no sense that this was gonna stir up

171
00:07:41.074 --> 00:07:42.294
any kind of opposition.

172
00:07:42.294 --> 00:07:45.130
Yeah it really was a culture clash though

173
00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.152
between the administration and these,

174
00:07:47.152 --> 00:07:50.511
wild-eyed professors who were

175
00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:53.890
way out on a limb with some of the ideas

176
00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.000
they want to do explore.

177
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.070
Well one of the ideas that they wanted to explore

178
00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.321
was that everything, everybody was playing games

179
00:07:59.321 --> 00:08:01.887
and that there was the professor game,

180
00:08:01.887 --> 00:08:04.170
and the ACA university game,

181
00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.893
well the university didn't like hearing that kind of stuff.

182
00:08:06.893 --> 00:08:09.450
So I think it was inevitable

183
00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.400
that there would be a big clash between them.

184
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.954
And you then began your experimental phase

185
00:08:15.954 --> 00:08:17.206
around that time as well.

186
00:08:17.206 --> 00:08:21.737
Also, I had read Aldous Huxley's book,

187
00:08:21.737 --> 00:08:24.340
"The Doors of Perception" about mescaline.

188
00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.983
And by chance he was at MIT.

189
00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:30.380
And that fall semester that I entered Harvard

190
00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:33.080
and he gave a series of lectures on visionary experience

191
00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.370
that were broadcasted on the Harvard radio station.

192
00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.490
And I listened to them and I wrote him a letter

193
00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.239
and asked him, where can I get mescaline?

194
00:08:39.239 --> 00:08:42.003
And he wrote back and gave me the name of chemical company.

195
00:08:42.003 --> 00:08:45.691
And anyway, eventually I was able to get mescaline

196
00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:48.160
and I experimented with it myself

197
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.180
and with friends over the next few years.

198
00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:55.193
And that was before almost anyone I knew had ever those.

199
00:08:55.193 --> 00:08:56.617
Right, right, right.

200
00:08:56.617 --> 00:08:59.960
Do you know, you probably know Deborah Szekely?

201
00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.590
Sure, oh yeah, she's amazing.

202
00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.163
She's incredible right?

203
00:09:05.567 --> 00:09:08.530
I spoke at Rancho La Puerta several years ago

204
00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:09.858
and I had her on the podcast.

205
00:09:09.858 --> 00:09:12.814
But when I was there, I kind of dug into the history

206
00:09:12.814 --> 00:09:15.522
of that place and what I was starting to learn about

207
00:09:15.522 --> 00:09:17.036
Edmund her late husband.

208
00:09:17.036 --> 00:09:19.567
I mean, Huxley used to go down there

209
00:09:19.567 --> 00:09:23.017
and it just sounded like a wild time at that moment.

210
00:09:23.017 --> 00:09:24.830
And talk about healthy aging.

211
00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.460
I mean, she's one of the most

212
00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.510
incredible.
She's 100 now, 99?

213
00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.810
Yeah something like that.

214
00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.233
Really, really impressive.

215
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.952
I mean, so interesting how that intersection

216
00:09:35.952 --> 00:09:39.196
of experimentation with psychedelics

217
00:09:39.196 --> 00:09:42.229
with kind of this emergent wellness culture,

218
00:09:42.229 --> 00:09:47.229
in its infancy to look back on that and to see

219
00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.063
kind of the fruits of that labor

220
00:09:49.063 --> 00:09:51.290
and the way that culture has kind of

221
00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.733
embraced these things now.

222
00:09:52.733 --> 00:09:54.500
It's crazy.

223
00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.944
I mean, it's taken a very long time,

224
00:09:55.944 --> 00:10:00.726
but I was just saying to someone yesterday that

225
00:10:00.726 --> 00:10:03.914
in the past few years I was doing a lot of traveling

226
00:10:03.914 --> 00:10:07.008
and lecturing around the country and in other countries.

227
00:10:07.008 --> 00:10:09.540
And no matter what the subject was

228
00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.014
I was talking about whether it was healthy aging, nutrition

229
00:10:12.014 --> 00:10:15.386
the questions I would get asked would be about psychedelics.

230
00:10:15.386 --> 00:10:17.440
That's why everyone wants to talk about.

231
00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.660
And then I saw I think it was this month

232
00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.700
or last month Vogue had a cover story

233
00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.680
on psilocybin mushrooms.

234
00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:25.513
I mean,

235
00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.530
That is mainstream.
(laughs)

236
00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:30.841
It's crazy.

237
00:10:30.841 --> 00:10:33.589
It's tricky for me.

238
00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:37.054
I mean, listen, just to preface what I'm gonna say

239
00:10:37.054 --> 00:10:40.110
I certainly acknowledged the tremendous amount

240
00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.861
of science that's occurring right now

241
00:10:41.861 --> 00:10:45.360
in terms of healing modalities, with respect to PTSD

242
00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.700
and depression and all the kind of clinical applications

243
00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:52.440
that we're discovering are appropriate for the substances.

244
00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.390
But I'm always challenged with how to square that

245
00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.130
with the fact that I'm a recovery guy

246
00:10:58.130 --> 00:10:59.888
like I've been on a recovery for a long time.

247
00:10:59.888 --> 00:11:04.670
And within that, the construct of that community

248
00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:06.040
it's very binary thing.

249
00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:07.189
Like you don't use substances.

250
00:11:07.189 --> 00:11:10.439
I never used psychedelic substances.

251
00:11:10.439 --> 00:11:14.440
I acknowledge these benefits that we're now seeing.

252
00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.552
It definitely flex with my mind a little bit,

253
00:11:17.552 --> 00:11:20.060
because I think did I try that

254
00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.814
and I've got a friend who's like a long time sober guy

255
00:11:22.814 --> 00:11:25.690
big book banger, all the way in.

256
00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.670
And he went and did a

257
00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.257
supervised siliciden experience with

258
00:11:35.082 --> 00:11:40.082
I don't know, whoever the kind of experts are in this field

259
00:11:40.303 --> 00:11:41.980
and it was profound for him.

260
00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:45.210
It will be hard really substances that you use

261
00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:48.818
in the way that people use alcohol or tobacco or cocaine,

262
00:11:48.818 --> 00:11:53.350
there things that people may take as on a single occasion

263
00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.090
and have some profound experience from.

264
00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:58.718
In my sense is that psychedelics

265
00:11:58.718 --> 00:12:01.580
they can show you possibilities.

266
00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.710
They don't give you any information

267
00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.580
on how to maintain the possibilities.

268
00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:06.150
And then you have to work on other ways

269
00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.740
to find out how to extend and maintain that.

270
00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.390
So they're not really things that you incorporate

271
00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:12.223
into your daily life.

272
00:12:12.223 --> 00:12:13.320
Right, right.

273
00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.300
Although with the microdosing

274
00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:16.580
and all that kind of stuff--
(words lost in cross talk)

275
00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:17.703
There's a lot of that going on.

276
00:12:17.703 --> 00:12:22.703
I guess my only reticence around the conversation is that

277
00:12:22.928 --> 00:12:24.602
the more it gets talked about,

278
00:12:24.602 --> 00:12:29.291
the more mainstream the kind of acceptance of it is,

279
00:12:29.291 --> 00:12:33.300
the more likely it is that people will be using it

280
00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.910
in ways that they shouldn't be.

281
00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:38.390
Sure, I think that's always a danger

282
00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:42.860
which is too bad because I think the the potential of them

283
00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.360
is really only realized that you mentioned certain setting

284
00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.193
at the beginning.

285
00:12:46.193 --> 00:12:48.209
If people have the right expectations

286
00:12:48.209 --> 00:12:50.020
and use them in the right circumstances,

287
00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.220
and otherwise, I think you just frittered away

288
00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.189
that potential.

289
00:12:54.189 --> 00:12:56.734
When you reflect back on those early

290
00:12:56.734 --> 00:12:59.240
experiences with psychedelics

291
00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.950
and kind of pair that with all the travel that you did.

292
00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.850
I mean how do you think about

293
00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.520
the impact of that on the kind of broader perspective

294
00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.106
that you've brought to the health conversation?

295
00:13:10.106 --> 00:13:12.683
First of all, I don't use these things anymore.

296
00:13:12.683 --> 00:13:15.178
I mean, they were things that I used in the past

297
00:13:15.178 --> 00:13:19.956
and on that book that I wrote "The Natural Mind"

298
00:13:19.956 --> 00:13:22.350
Alan Watts wrote a blurb for it.

299
00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.780
And one of the things he said was that,

300
00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:29.351
"When you get the message you can hang up the telephone."

301
00:13:29.351 --> 00:13:33.040
And I think that really applies to these experiences.

302
00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:34.880
So there's a lot of stuff I learned from them.

303
00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.790
I don't really feel the need to repeat those experiences.

304
00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:42.790
But one thing that I saw very powerfully is that

305
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:45.550
what goes on in your mind really determines

306
00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:48.528
and shapes your experience of the external world.

307
00:13:48.528 --> 00:13:52.350
And I've applied that very much in my work with patients,

308
00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.680
because I just find over and over again,

309
00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.710
that the root causes of illness

310
00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.920
are often in the mental, emotional sphere

311
00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.601
and not in the physical body or they're in both.

312
00:14:02.601 --> 00:14:05.228
But conventional medicine has so ignored

313
00:14:05.228 --> 00:14:08.420
that non-physical part of ourselves.

314
00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:10.150
And I think that's a great limitation.

315
00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.515
I mean, I think one of the things

316
00:14:12.515 --> 00:14:17.515
I appreciate about you the most is just that level

317
00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:22.880
of cognizance and emphasis that you put on the mind

318
00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.030
and your willingness to delve deep into the nature

319
00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:29.262
of consciousness, and these are just anathema

320
00:14:29.262 --> 00:14:31.423
in conventional medical practice.

321
00:14:31.423 --> 00:14:33.793
It just doesn't compute.

322
00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.206
One of the most interesting courses I ever took

323
00:14:36.206 --> 00:14:39.905
was in medical hypnosis at Columbia University

324
00:14:39.905 --> 00:14:42.094
(indistinct) for Physicians.

325
00:14:42.094 --> 00:14:45.490
I learned so many fascinating things there.

326
00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:49.180
And as a result of that, I made mind-body medicine

327
00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:52.440
a major component of the integrative medicine curriculum

328
00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:53.273
that I developed.

329
00:14:53.273 --> 00:14:55.750
And I very frequently refer patients

330
00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:59.791
to mind-body practitioners, whether that's hypnotherapists

331
00:14:59.791 --> 00:15:02.730
or visualization practitioners or any one

332
00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.700
of a number of other modalities

333
00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.486
because I find these things to be incredibly effective,

334
00:15:07.486 --> 00:15:08.589
cost-effective,

335
00:15:08.589 --> 00:15:11.917
I mean, even fun for both practitioner and patient--

336
00:15:11.917 --> 00:15:14.236
And relatively free of risk.

337
00:15:14.236 --> 00:15:18.420
Yeah totally. And they are so under utilized in medicine.

338
00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.730
And I remember once I was challenged

339
00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:23.930
to a public debate by Arnold Relman

340
00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.009
who was the editor of the New England Journal of Medicine.

341
00:15:26.009 --> 00:15:29.470
And it was a very publicized event.

342
00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:33.340
And I mean, I have never met a more closed minded person.

343
00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.563
His position was all you have to do is show me the evidence.

344
00:15:36.563 --> 00:15:38.900
You know, you show me the evidence, blah, blah, blah.

345
00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:40.617
So anything you'd show him, he'd say,

346
00:15:40.617 --> 00:15:42.387
"Oh, well that's not evidence."

347
00:15:42.387 --> 00:15:44.270
And one of the questions that I asked him

348
00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.044
I said that, "The best research area

349
00:15:46.044 --> 00:15:49.000
of what I do is mind-body interactions.

350
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.920
There's been 30 years of studies on that.

351
00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.123
And yet these modalities are so under utilized

352
00:15:55.123 --> 00:15:57.457
what would you do to increase their use?"

353
00:15:57.457 --> 00:15:58.467
And his response was,

354
00:15:58.467 --> 00:16:00.560
"There is no evidence for mind-body interactions."

355
00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:05.560
So, I don't know what you can do with that.

356
00:16:05.655 --> 00:16:10.655
There's a profound hubris around

357
00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.140
the scientific method.

358
00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:13.142
Science is truth.

359
00:16:13.142 --> 00:16:16.920
Science is fact, but that overlooks the fact that

360
00:16:18.142 --> 00:16:19.829
it's a method.

361
00:16:19.829 --> 00:16:22.680
It's not a fact in and of itself.

362
00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:25.320
It's a means of approximating truth.

363
00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.190
It's a way to approach or discover truth,

364
00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:30.800
but it's also not the sole method and it's flawed.

365
00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.597
And I think there's so much arrogance around.

366
00:16:32.597 --> 00:16:34.670
This is the way that we do it.

367
00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:39.670
And that kind of approach is very dualistic or binary

368
00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:45.040
in that it's controlling for this variable or this and that.

369
00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:47.770
And obviously we've made, I'm not dismissing it.

370
00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.990
It's like this is the engine of progress of course,

371
00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.500
but it overlooks the holistic nature

372
00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:56.033
of the human body and the interplay

373
00:16:56.033 --> 00:16:58.304
between all of these very sensitive systems

374
00:16:58.304 --> 00:17:01.680
And science and medicine, in our part of the world

375
00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.004
are totally dominated by materialism.

376
00:17:04.004 --> 00:17:06.387
You know a philosophy that says that

377
00:17:06.387 --> 00:17:09.276
the only thing that's real is that which can be seen

378
00:17:09.276 --> 00:17:11.268
and touched and measured.

379
00:17:11.268 --> 00:17:15.052
And that if you see a change in a physical system

380
00:17:15.052 --> 00:17:16.561
the cause has to be physical,

381
00:17:16.561 --> 00:17:20.668
non-physical causation of physical events, doesn't compute.

382
00:17:20.668 --> 00:17:21.717
It's not allowed for.

383
00:17:21.717 --> 00:17:25.843
So in hypnosis there are so many demonstrations

384
00:17:25.843 --> 00:17:29.592
like you can touch a person with a finger

385
00:17:29.592 --> 00:17:33.194
and you have them believe it's a piece of red hot metal

386
00:17:33.194 --> 00:17:35.749
and if they're in a deep trance they get an actual blister.

387
00:17:35.749 --> 00:17:39.050
I mean, that is non-physical causation of a physical event.

388
00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:43.199
So if you try to get a doctor to look at that or scientists,

389
00:17:43.199 --> 00:17:46.543
it's just a curious thing it doesn't have any meaning.

390
00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:52.220
That kind of makes me think of your interesting ideas

391
00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:53.529
around the placebo effect

392
00:17:53.529 --> 00:17:56.510
and the way that we kind of think about that backwards.

393
00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.330
Fortunately, that's one of the things that's changing now,

394
00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:01.160
which makes me very happy,

395
00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:04.760
but still the most common ways I hear the word placebo used

396
00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:06.240
are in phrases.

397
00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.550
Like how do you know that's not just a placebo effect

398
00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:10.992
and the most interesting word there is just

399
00:18:10.992 --> 00:18:14.938
or we have to rule out the placebo effect.

400
00:18:14.938 --> 00:18:17.410
That's what we wanna rule in.

401
00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:20.031
The placebo response is pure healing from within

402
00:18:20.031 --> 00:18:23.560
mediated, by the mind unmixed up with the direct effects

403
00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.413
of treatment which are likely to be harmful.

404
00:18:25.413 --> 00:18:29.030
I think I've heard you talk about the fact that

405
00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.975
in every kind of double blind trial.

406
00:18:31.975 --> 00:18:34.873
If you look at the placebo group,

407
00:18:34.873 --> 00:18:37.101
there's always a few people

408
00:18:37.101 --> 00:18:40.228
that have experienced the result as if they have been

409
00:18:40.228 --> 00:18:42.820
taking the medication.
All of the changes exactly.

410
00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:44.550
And that's the most interesting thing--

411
00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.704
Amazing, that any change we can produce

412
00:18:46.704 --> 00:18:50.100
with a pharmacological agent can be exactly mimicked

413
00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:51.777
in at least some people, some of the time,

414
00:18:51.777 --> 00:18:53.803
by a mind mediated mechanism.

415
00:18:53.803 --> 00:18:56.150
So fortunately the change that's happened

416
00:18:56.150 --> 00:18:57.230
and this is a result

417
00:18:57.230 --> 00:18:58.948
of these new brain imaging technologies

418
00:18:58.948 --> 00:19:03.830
is that now you can show that certain parts of the brain

419
00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:05.637
are active when people have placebo responses

420
00:19:05.637 --> 00:19:10.080
and this makes it accessible and real to people.

421
00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.400
So I think placebos are being taken much more seriously.

422
00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:16.540
Because now the imaging science has caught up

423
00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:18.460
and neuroscience has progressed to the point

424
00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:19.339
where they can provide

425
00:19:19.339 --> 00:19:23.317
an explanation that makes sense to the conventional

426
00:19:23.317 --> 00:19:24.150
and community.
Exactly.

427
00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.877
And same exactly with meditation,

428
00:19:25.877 --> 00:19:28.547
or there've been a tremendous number of studies

429
00:19:28.547 --> 00:19:31.270
of very long time series meditators

430
00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:32.630
showing different activity,

431
00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:35.240
in different brain areas as a result.

432
00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:38.020
So the fact that a meditation practice

433
00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:39.540
can actually physically change the brain.

434
00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.283
I mean, that's really interesting stuff.

435
00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:46.960
But within that, there's this profound,

436
00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:49.504
deep appreciation for the mystical, right?

437
00:19:49.504 --> 00:19:50.337
Yeah.

438
00:19:50.337 --> 00:19:52.160
Which is like my favorite thing to talk about.

439
00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:52.993
Oh good.

440
00:19:52.993 --> 00:19:53.826
(Andrew laughs)

441
00:19:53.826 --> 00:19:56.580
And I suspect that was in part informed

442
00:19:56.580 --> 00:20:00.468
by all these shamans and various healing practitioners

443
00:20:00.468 --> 00:20:02.282
that you've experienced over the decades

444
00:20:02.282 --> 00:20:04.990
in south America and Asia, et cetera.

445
00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:09.894
One of the attitudes that I just can't stand is that

446
00:20:09.894 --> 00:20:12.870
when scientists believe that it's their job

447
00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:14.127
to make mystery go away.

448
00:20:14.127 --> 00:20:17.309
It seems to me, mystery is an essential aspect

449
00:20:17.309 --> 00:20:21.610
of the universe and that you have to appreciate that

450
00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:22.743
and wonder at it.

451
00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:26.307
Once Terence McKenna, I once heard say that,

452
00:20:26.307 --> 00:20:28.800
"The bigger you build the fire

453
00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.821
the more you're aware of the extent of the darkness."

454
00:20:30.821 --> 00:20:33.360
And I think that's true.

455
00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:34.193
And,

456
00:20:35.159 --> 00:20:40.159
I think that at the heart of our experience is mystery,

457
00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.372
that stuff that we can appreciate

458
00:20:42.372 --> 00:20:44.744
but we can't really understand with our mind.

459
00:20:44.744 --> 00:20:49.744
So how do you get a typical doctor to develop

460
00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:55.640
a broader appreciation for that?

461
00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:56.800
How does that work?

462
00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.190
I mean, that's kind of at the crux of your thing.

463
00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:00.790
Well, I train doctors.

464
00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:02.470
At the center I have at the University of Arizona,

465
00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:03.303
this is what we do.

466
00:21:03.303 --> 00:21:05.933
We're the world leaders in integrated medical education.

467
00:21:05.933 --> 00:21:08.560
And we train doctors and nurse practitioners

468
00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:11.320
and medical students and residents,

469
00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:15.478
and our main training is a two year intensive fellowship.

470
00:21:15.478 --> 00:21:19.149
We've now graduated over 2000 physicians from that

471
00:21:19.149 --> 00:21:20.925
in all specialties, all ages.

472
00:21:20.925 --> 00:21:23.140
And it's all the things they should've learned

473
00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.847
in medical school but didn't, including like healing

474
00:21:25.847 --> 00:21:27.886
and the fact the body can heal itself and so forth.

475
00:21:27.886 --> 00:21:30.052
So the people who come are self-selected

476
00:21:30.052 --> 00:21:32.040
and what's happening today is that

477
00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:35.320
so many people in medicine are just burned out.

478
00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:38.082
They're so disillusioned that what medicine has become.

479
00:21:38.082 --> 00:21:40.980
And they know there's gotta be a different way

480
00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:43.810
and so they come to us

481
00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:45.487
and there's a new generation out there,

482
00:21:45.487 --> 00:21:47.627
I think that gets it in a different way.

483
00:21:47.627 --> 00:21:48.580
There was

484
00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:54.160
a social club that I belong to at Harvard Medical School

485
00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:57.640
that had a motto that I always really liked.

486
00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:00.307
It was, "We dress the wound, God heals it."

487
00:22:01.575 --> 00:22:05.827
And that's acknowledging that aspect of things.

488
00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:11.240
I mean, on the surface, obviously you've cut your hand

489
00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:13.030
or whatever, you can watch it heal.

490
00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:14.878
Like there is something going on that you don't

491
00:22:14.878 --> 00:22:18.910
need to really attend to the body will take care of itself.

492
00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:20.864
But beyond that, we don't really appreciate

493
00:22:20.864 --> 00:22:23.700
the extent to which the body is constantly doing that.

494
00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.250
Yeah and I find in my work,

495
00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.000
one of the things that I have to do

496
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.370
is try to instill greater confidence in people

497
00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:32.860
about their body's ability to take care of things.

498
00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:36.010
You know, most people have no awareness of that

499
00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:37.348
and think they've got to run off for help

500
00:22:37.348 --> 00:22:39.610
as the first thing.

501
00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:43.580
And what's interesting is that these are things that have

502
00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.729
been paramount in all kinds of modalities

503
00:22:45.729 --> 00:22:49.090
dating back through history that were very dismissive

504
00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:51.714
of from Ayurveda the Chinese medicine and all,

505
00:22:51.714 --> 00:22:54.220
I mean now I feel like we're kind of looking

506
00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:57.974
at those things with an appropriate level of reverence

507
00:22:57.974 --> 00:23:01.090
and critique to figure out what works and what doesn't.

508
00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:01.923
Exactly.

509
00:23:01.923 --> 00:23:02.988
But these are not new ideas.

510
00:23:02.988 --> 00:23:04.115
(Andrew laughs)

511
00:23:04.115 --> 00:23:05.618
Yeah. Hardly.

512
00:23:05.618 --> 00:23:07.260
(both laughing)

513
00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.333
I mean, you've been in it for 50 years

514
00:23:09.333 --> 00:23:11.600
but this has been going on for thousands of years.

515
00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:14.905
It bugs me when I hear doctors

516
00:23:14.905 --> 00:23:17.435
refer to what they're doing as traditional medicine.

517
00:23:17.435 --> 00:23:20.725
To me, traditional medicine is Chinese medicine,

518
00:23:20.725 --> 00:23:23.290
Native American medicine, Ayurvedic medicine,

519
00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:24.690
things that are thousands of years old.

520
00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:27.560
I mean, our system of medicine in its present form

521
00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:30.114
is maybe a hundred years old, 120 at the most

522
00:23:30.114 --> 00:23:32.023
it doesn't go back very far.

523
00:23:32.023 --> 00:23:36.076
But packed into that is a certain level of arrogance.

524
00:23:36.076 --> 00:23:38.990
And this idea that we're always

525
00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:40.492
at the pinnacle of human understanding.

526
00:23:40.492 --> 00:23:44.200
And you don't understand because we have this training

527
00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:45.377
and we're gonna tell you to do,

528
00:23:45.377 --> 00:23:47.239
that's starting to shift right now.

529
00:23:47.239 --> 00:23:51.772
But that arrogance, that hubris becomes

530
00:23:51.772 --> 00:23:55.197
a bar to having a growth mindset

531
00:23:55.197 --> 00:23:57.958
or an openness to other ideas.

532
00:23:57.958 --> 00:24:02.958
Very well put, and to me, the essence of science

533
00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.626
is open-minded observation.

534
00:24:05.626 --> 00:24:07.003
That's where you start.

535
00:24:08.931 --> 00:24:11.322
I've been re-watching, "The Knick" did you

536
00:24:11.322 --> 00:24:12.677
watch "The Knick"?
Yes I did yeah.

537
00:24:12.677 --> 00:24:14.500
"The Knick" is like the greatest thing on tel

538
00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.633
it's unbelievable how good this show is.

539
00:24:16.633 --> 00:24:19.110
And for those that don't know,

540
00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:21.967
it's about Clive Owen plays this surgeon in 1900.

541
00:24:21.967 --> 00:24:25.917
Thackery he's full of vim and vigor and ego and arrogance.

542
00:24:25.917 --> 00:24:29.571
And on some level he is a genius, but he's on the edge.

543
00:24:29.571 --> 00:24:34.337
He's a fanatical cocaine addict, opium addict,

544
00:24:34.337 --> 00:24:38.336
but he kind of rules the roost in "The Knick" this hospital.

545
00:24:38.336 --> 00:24:40.540
And what I didn't realize until I looked

546
00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.190
into it a little bit more deeply

547
00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:45.355
is that he's based on William Stewart Halsted.

548
00:24:45.355 --> 00:24:46.188
Did you know that?

549
00:24:46.188 --> 00:24:47.080
I did not know that.

550
00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:50.368
Who has kind of a similar trajectory having been

551
00:24:50.368 --> 00:24:53.151
a cocaine addict and the guy who kind of set in motion

552
00:24:53.151 --> 00:24:58.039
this residency program that is a legacy today

553
00:24:58.039 --> 00:25:00.930
of residents working insane hours.

554
00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:03.370
And a lot of that was dictated because the guy was like

555
00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:04.498
on cocaine all the time.

556
00:25:04.498 --> 00:25:07.160
And he could like work around the clock.

557
00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.789
But I bring it up because that arrogance.

558
00:25:10.789 --> 00:25:14.050
He's like, "I'm the guy who's in charge, I know all."

559
00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:18.022
And when you look back on it, it's comedic because

560
00:25:18.022 --> 00:25:21.180
there he's giving people turpentine and soaking their feet

561
00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.041
in mercury and doing all kinds of crazy stuff.

562
00:25:23.041 --> 00:25:25.890
That's just preposterous.

563
00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.530
But it makes you think like a hundred years from now,

564
00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:29.510
what are we gonna look back on?

565
00:25:29.510 --> 00:25:32.430
What we're doing in this moment and realize

566
00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:34.268
how insane it is.

567
00:25:34.268 --> 00:25:38.411
I gave an invited lecture a few years ago that I called,

568
00:25:38.411 --> 00:25:39.862
What We're Rethinking.

569
00:25:39.862 --> 00:25:43.572
And I looked at examples in the past 50 years

570
00:25:43.572 --> 00:25:46.480
of practices that now we look back on

571
00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:48.149
and I can't believe we did them,

572
00:25:48.149 --> 00:25:50.693
but leaving people with a question of what are we doing now

573
00:25:50.693 --> 00:25:52.700
that 50 years from now look back the same way.

574
00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:56.140
So some of them were one is there were floor scopes

575
00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.170
and shoe stores when I was growing up

576
00:25:58.170 --> 00:25:59.101
to check the fit of shoes.

577
00:25:59.101 --> 00:26:01.564
So there was this big console

578
00:26:01.564 --> 00:26:05.780
and you'd stick your feet with your new shoes onto them.

579
00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:07.122
And there was a big glowing green screen

580
00:26:07.122 --> 00:26:09.042
and you'd see the bones of your feet

581
00:26:09.042 --> 00:26:12.530
and the game as a kid was to distract your parents

582
00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:13.590
and the shoe salesman.

583
00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:16.730
So you could spend as much time under there as possible.

584
00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:17.563
I mean, unbelievable.

585
00:26:17.563 --> 00:26:18.557
More like an x-ray?

586
00:26:18.557 --> 00:26:20.910
Yeah, like an x-ray in a shoe store.

587
00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:22.000
(laughs)

588
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:26.540
So another one was, this is much worse.

589
00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:29.620
There was the thymus behind the breast bone

590
00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:33.260
is the master gland of the immune system.

591
00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:36.015
It's where immune cells go to be trained

592
00:26:36.015 --> 00:26:39.635
to recognize antigens and foreign things.

593
00:26:39.635 --> 00:26:42.170
And it's very active in childhood

594
00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:44.249
and in adolescence and shrinks in adolescence.

595
00:26:44.249 --> 00:26:45.690
When I was in medical school

596
00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:47.060
nobody knew what the thymus did.

597
00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:51.094
It was considered a functionless organ, a vestigial organ.

598
00:26:51.094 --> 00:26:52.670
Well, first of all, that's arrogance.

599
00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:54.290
You don't know what the function

600
00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:55.360
of something in the body is

601
00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.680
so you say it has no function.

602
00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.969
And then the next step is that, that allows you then

603
00:26:58.969 --> 00:27:01.659
to destroy it or take it out.

604
00:27:01.659 --> 00:27:04.193
And so doctors and this was in the 1950s,

605
00:27:05.304 --> 00:27:09.210
invented a disease that every child had

606
00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.655
called phonemic hypertrophy meaning the thymus is too large

607
00:27:11.655 --> 00:27:14.750
treatable by bombarding it with x-rays,

608
00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:16.181
which causes it to shrink immediately.

609
00:27:16.181 --> 00:27:20.612
I had a good friend who was a graduate

610
00:27:20.612 --> 00:27:22.800
in my undergraduate class.

611
00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:23.897
And then he went to Harvard Law School.

612
00:27:23.897 --> 00:27:25.945
He was clerking for a Supreme court clerk

613
00:27:25.945 --> 00:27:27.690
and he got a letter one day

614
00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.200
from Michael Reese Hospital in Chicago,

615
00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:30.861
very good hospital in Chicago

616
00:27:30.861 --> 00:27:33.211
saying our records show that as a child

617
00:27:33.211 --> 00:27:34.810
you were taken to our clinic

618
00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:37.002
for a course of x-rays directed at your thymus.

619
00:27:37.002 --> 00:27:39.620
And we're now finding that people that had this

620
00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:42.957
treatment are developing thyroid cancer at unusual rates.

621
00:27:42.957 --> 00:27:45.000
And they urged him to come in and get checked.

622
00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.650
He didn't even know this had been done to him.

623
00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:51.587
But I mean unbelievable that kind of arrogance.

624
00:27:51.587 --> 00:27:55.187
When I think about the traditional modalities

625
00:27:55.187 --> 00:27:58.224
around healthcare, one thing I'm pretty certain

626
00:27:58.224 --> 00:28:01.844
we're not gonna look kindly on years from now is

627
00:28:01.844 --> 00:28:06.844
the myopic perspective on lifestyle, diet, nutrition,

628
00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.836
all of these things that are the key contributors

629
00:28:09.836 --> 00:28:14.720
to the real epidemics of our time,

630
00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:16.483
which are the chronic lifestyle illnesses

631
00:28:16.483 --> 00:28:19.399
that are killing more people than anything else right now.

632
00:28:19.399 --> 00:28:21.300
And the overmedication of everybody

633
00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.750
the treatment of symptoms, the inability to really get

634
00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:28.550
to the underlying causes and to address those

635
00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:29.383
in a meaningful way.

636
00:28:29.383 --> 00:28:32.750
A major component of integrative medicine

637
00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:33.763
is lifestyle medicine.

638
00:28:33.763 --> 00:28:36.720
And I think that puts us in a really strong position

639
00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.635
to offer real preventive advice to people.

640
00:28:38.635 --> 00:28:41.770
The total instruction I got in nutrition

641
00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:44.902
in four years at Harvard Medical School was 30 minutes.

642
00:28:44.902 --> 00:28:46.040
Which we're grudging--

643
00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:46.930
Haven't changed much.

644
00:28:46.930 --> 00:28:48.370
No it hasn't changed much.

645
00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:51.240
And when nutrition is taught, it's taught as biochemistry

646
00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:52.480
which is forgotten as soon as

647
00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.146
the biochemistry exams are over.

648
00:28:54.146 --> 00:28:58.240
So I think most most doctors are functionally illiterate

649
00:28:58.240 --> 00:28:59.950
in nutrition unless they've made an effort

650
00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:02.515
to learn it on their own, which is not so easy to do.

651
00:29:02.515 --> 00:29:06.750
Well, systemically the education is not about

652
00:29:07.594 --> 00:29:11.290
preventing disease or the promotion of wellbeing.

653
00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.372
It's about the treatment of disease.

654
00:29:13.372 --> 00:29:17.036
In fact, that was one of my motivations for getting out

655
00:29:17.036 --> 00:29:18.809
and learning other methods was

656
00:29:18.809 --> 00:29:22.050
I had learned nothing about health and healing

657
00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.105
in my medical studies.

658
00:29:23.105 --> 00:29:25.520
No, I hardly ever heard the word healing used.

659
00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.156
And it always seemed to me the main job,

660
00:29:28.156 --> 00:29:31.050
my main job was to teach people how not to get sick

661
00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:31.883
in the first place.

662
00:29:31.883 --> 00:29:34.053
And I hadn't learned how to do that.

663
00:29:34.053 --> 00:29:37.670
So let's define integrative medicine.

664
00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:39.100
Well the short definition is

665
00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:40.810
it's the intelligent combination of

666
00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:44.750
conventional medicine and natural and preventive medicine.

667
00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:47.152
Then the selected use of alternative therapies.

668
00:29:47.152 --> 00:29:50.100
But I think the fuller definition is

669
00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:52.328
it's a system that really focuses

670
00:29:52.328 --> 00:29:54.679
on the body's ability to heal itself.

671
00:29:54.679 --> 00:29:56.839
That looks at people as whole persons

672
00:29:56.839 --> 00:29:58.399
not just physical bodies

673
00:29:58.399 --> 00:30:01.833
that takes all aspects of lifestyle into account

674
00:30:01.833 --> 00:30:04.974
that values the practitioner-patient relationship

675
00:30:04.974 --> 00:30:07.134
and makes use of all available treatments

676
00:30:07.134 --> 00:30:09.110
no matter where they come from

677
00:30:09.110 --> 00:30:10.450
as long as they're not gonna hurt people

678
00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:13.270
and show reasonable evidence of efficacy.

679
00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:15.361
And how does that differ from functional medicine?

680
00:30:15.361 --> 00:30:18.298
Functional medicine, there is some overlap

681
00:30:18.298 --> 00:30:20.620
between functional medicine and integrative medicine

682
00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:21.767
but I think they're quite different.

683
00:30:21.767 --> 00:30:25.511
I think that functional medicine is very focused

684
00:30:25.511 --> 00:30:27.214
on biochemical mechanisms.

685
00:30:27.214 --> 00:30:30.216
I think overly focused on that.

686
00:30:30.216 --> 00:30:33.132
And some of those biochemical mechanisms

687
00:30:33.132 --> 00:30:35.782
I think may not have great clinical relevance.

688
00:30:35.782 --> 00:30:40.425
And then in practice, their treatment is correcting

689
00:30:40.425 --> 00:30:45.425
these functional biochemical disturbances

690
00:30:45.719 --> 00:30:49.977
by recommending a lot of supplements to take.

691
00:30:49.977 --> 00:30:52.438
I think that functional medicine

692
00:30:52.438 --> 00:30:55.820
has much less emphasis on mind-body medicine

693
00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:57.580
on spirituality and medicine

694
00:30:57.580 --> 00:30:58.871
on the community aspects of medicine.

695
00:30:58.871 --> 00:31:03.130
So I personally think that integrative medicine

696
00:31:03.130 --> 00:31:05.482
is more comprehensive more robust system.

697
00:31:05.482 --> 00:31:09.121
So in the training of this next generation

698
00:31:09.121 --> 00:31:12.443
of integrative medicine practitioners,

699
00:31:13.941 --> 00:31:16.725
there's a lot of challenges

700
00:31:16.725 --> 00:31:20.430
but not the least of which is what is the business model?

701
00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:22.242
because your butting up against a conventional system

702
00:31:22.242 --> 00:31:25.903
that's driven by insurance reimbursed

703
00:31:25.903 --> 00:31:26.961
By profit.

704
00:31:26.961 --> 00:31:27.933
Yeah exactly.

705
00:31:27.933 --> 00:31:31.402
You know, as dysfunctional as our healthcare system is,

706
00:31:31.402 --> 00:31:35.061
and it's very dysfunctional, it's generating rivers of money

707
00:31:35.061 --> 00:31:37.751
and that money is flowing at the very few pockets.

708
00:31:37.751 --> 00:31:40.053
It's the pockets of the big pharmaceutical companies

709
00:31:40.053 --> 00:31:43.617
the manufacturers of medical devices, the big insurers

710
00:31:43.617 --> 00:31:47.089
and the big healthcare, corporations.

711
00:31:47.089 --> 00:31:50.572
And those vested interests don't want anything to change.

712
00:31:50.572 --> 00:31:53.114
They're making out like bandits.

713
00:31:53.114 --> 00:31:56.930
And I feel bad we train the people that come to us

714
00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:59.270
in a really highly and then we turn them out in the world

715
00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:00.443
where everything's stacked against them.

716
00:32:00.443 --> 00:32:03.957
And all the priorities of reimbursement are backward.

717
00:32:03.957 --> 00:32:07.280
We don't pay a doctor to sit with a patient

718
00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:08.302
and teach a breathing exercise.

719
00:32:08.302 --> 00:32:11.578
We pay a doctor to prescribe drugs,

720
00:32:11.578 --> 00:32:16.068
and often when the doctor doesn't think those are necessary

721
00:32:16.068 --> 00:32:17.265
or they do tests,

722
00:32:17.265 --> 00:32:21.250
we don't reimburse for lifestyle counseling.

723
00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:23.027
So that all has to change.

724
00:32:23.027 --> 00:32:25.360
There has been some progress.

725
00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.480
I know Dean Ornish recently got a insurance reimbursement

726
00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:32.300
for some of his programs, but that's the key, right?

727
00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.127
You've got to get insurance on board with those.

728
00:32:34.127 --> 00:32:37.230
Otherwise it doesn't make financial sense

729
00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:40.630
especially when a big part of this is follow-up

730
00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:43.221
and outreach and accountability, where you have,

731
00:32:43.221 --> 00:32:45.812
kind of other practitioners in your office

732
00:32:45.812 --> 00:32:47.289
who are checking in on everybody

733
00:32:47.289 --> 00:32:49.390
and making sure that they're staying on track

734
00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:51.476
because you can't just say, you need to go

735
00:32:51.476 --> 00:32:54.410
here's the diet you need, or here's the exercise routine

736
00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:55.822
and expect people to actually follow that

737
00:32:55.822 --> 00:33:00.822
unless they feel, that there's some kind of program

738
00:33:01.043 --> 00:33:04.090
or emotional connection to it.

739
00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:07.387
Totally, and I think the challenge is that

740
00:33:07.387 --> 00:33:12.387
we have to generate data that we can take the people who pay

741
00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:16.484
for healthcare to show them that it's in their interest

742
00:33:16.484 --> 00:33:17.793
to pay for these.

743
00:33:17.793 --> 00:33:19.332
(indistinct)

744
00:33:19.332 --> 00:33:22.950
Ultimately it's, if you keep these people

745
00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:25.092
from getting diabetes and becoming obese

746
00:33:25.092 --> 00:33:27.665
it's gonna be a far less strain

747
00:33:27.665 --> 00:33:30.344
than the insurance mechanism.

748
00:33:30.344 --> 00:33:32.900
Apropos of that.

749
00:33:32.900 --> 00:33:34.950
I remember seeing a few years ago

750
00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:36.077
there was a week long series

751
00:33:36.077 --> 00:33:39.070
in the New York times about the impact

752
00:33:39.070 --> 00:33:41.298
of the type two diabetes epidemic in New York city.

753
00:33:41.298 --> 00:33:45.199
And one of the articles pointed out

754
00:33:45.199 --> 00:33:50.199
that for every preventive nutritional counseling session

755
00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:55.157
that a diabetes center did in New York that the center lost,

756
00:33:55.157 --> 00:33:57.881
I think on average something like 50 to $70.

757
00:33:57.881 --> 00:33:58.714
(laughs)

758
00:33:58.714 --> 00:34:03.421
For every amputation of a diabetic limb, they made 6,000.

759
00:34:03.421 --> 00:34:05.400
So it's a nice alignment of incentives.

760
00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:07.600
Exactly, exactly.

761
00:34:07.600 --> 00:34:09.244
I mean, that's a systemic change.

762
00:34:09.244 --> 00:34:11.300
It's great to train these doctors

763
00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:12.680
and put them out in the world.

764
00:34:12.680 --> 00:34:15.446
And I believe that anybody, maybe not all people

765
00:34:15.446 --> 00:34:17.520
but most people who go to medical school

766
00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:18.640
are well-intentioned they wanna help people.

767
00:34:18.640 --> 00:34:19.956
Absolutely and in fact,

768
00:34:19.956 --> 00:34:22.420
a lot of the people that come to us for training

769
00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:24.094
say that this has really restored

770
00:34:24.094 --> 00:34:26.870
why they went into medicine in the first place.

771
00:34:26.870 --> 00:34:28.152
Yeah, because there's so much disillusionment.

772
00:34:28.152 --> 00:34:29.720
(words lost in cross talk)
Tremendously.

773
00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:32.012
And that's a huge change when I was in medical school

774
00:34:32.012 --> 00:34:34.884
medicine looked like a very desirable profession.

775
00:34:34.884 --> 00:34:38.326
Doctors heads great status in society.

776
00:34:38.326 --> 00:34:41.070
But I think one of the great promises was that

777
00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:42.300
it offered you autonomy.

778
00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:44.456
You could be your own boss, no longer.

779
00:34:44.456 --> 00:34:47.828
I mean, doctors can not make it in solo practice anymore.

780
00:34:47.828 --> 00:34:50.060
You have to work for a corporation that tells you

781
00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:51.551
how many patients you have to see an hour

782
00:34:51.551 --> 00:34:54.163
that tells you what you can do and not do

783
00:34:54.163 --> 00:34:56.758
in the way of recommending treatments.

784
00:34:56.758 --> 00:34:58.060
That's all changed.

785
00:34:58.060 --> 00:34:59.419
I can't tell you how many doctors I've met

786
00:34:59.419 --> 00:35:01.260
in the past few years who say they wished

787
00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:02.831
