00:46:15.846 --> 00:46:18.430
And then it becomes a conversation around

1046
00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:21.551
how do you do that without exogenous substances.

1047
00:46:21.551 --> 00:46:23.873
I think obviously it's possible,

1048
00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:28.220
with Tony Robbins and that firewalk

1049
00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:29.460
I wasn't taking anything.

1050
00:46:29.460 --> 00:46:31.364
It was the group energy that he created

1051
00:46:31.364 --> 00:46:33.047
that caused the change.

1052
00:46:33.047 --> 00:46:36.169
Yeah but it's impossible

1053
00:46:36.169 --> 00:46:38.727
to not develop a deeper appreciation

1054
00:46:38.727 --> 00:46:41.453
for just how powerful the mind is.

1055
00:46:42.520 --> 00:46:47.520
And then it becomes even more inexcusable in my perspective,

1056
00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:50.927
from the lack of appreciation for that.

1057
00:46:50.927 --> 00:46:55.927
And in medical and in the mental health space

1058
00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:58.610
where we're just over-medicating people like crazy,

1059
00:46:58.610 --> 00:47:00.645
especially kids, the extent to which

1060
00:47:00.645 --> 00:47:02.974
they're prescribing SSRI
The vast experiments--

1061
00:47:02.974 --> 00:47:05.150
being done with kids.

1062
00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:07.380
We use drugs for ADHD and everything like that.

1063
00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:10.870
We have no idea what these do to the developing brain.

1064
00:47:10.870 --> 00:47:15.860
And it's ironic that the word psychiatry comes from Greek

1065
00:47:15.860 --> 00:47:18.669
it means soul doctoring, what a great concept.

1066
00:47:18.669 --> 00:47:21.434
But it's ironic that of all the medical specialties

1067
00:47:21.434 --> 00:47:23.897
psychiatry is the one that's become most

1068
00:47:23.897 --> 00:47:26.283
mired in materialism.

1069
00:47:27.172 --> 00:47:31.754
The dogma is that all disordered thinking and emotion

1070
00:47:31.754 --> 00:47:34.576
is the result of disordered brain biochemistry.

1071
00:47:34.576 --> 00:47:37.020
And the only way to deal with that

1072
00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:39.806
is by giving pharmaceuticals to change that.

1073
00:47:39.806 --> 00:47:43.640
And if that were right, if that were true,

1074
00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:45.330
we would be much more effective

1075
00:47:45.330 --> 00:47:47.322
at dealing with mental illness.

1076
00:47:47.322 --> 00:47:48.591
They don't seem to work very well.

1077
00:47:48.591 --> 00:47:51.264
No. That's a very good observation.

1078
00:47:51.264 --> 00:47:54.399
If you look at a psychiatry journals

1079
00:47:54.399 --> 00:47:56.607
and look at the ads for the pharmaceuticals,

1080
00:47:56.607 --> 00:47:59.374
you would think that depression, anxiety,

1081
00:47:59.374 --> 00:48:03.536
that these would be things of the past, and it's not so.

1082
00:48:03.536 --> 00:48:06.004
They are not very effective these methods.

1083
00:48:06.004 --> 00:48:10.020
I also think that there's something going on culturally,

1084
00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:14.350
where we've been lulled into this belief

1085
00:48:14.350 --> 00:48:17.010
that we should never be anything but happy.

1086
00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:18.721
I mean you wrote a whole book about this.

1087
00:48:18.721 --> 00:48:19.554
(laughs)

1088
00:48:19.554 --> 00:48:21.624
Like, if I'm not feeling good today

1089
00:48:21.624 --> 00:48:23.564
the first impulse is to

1090
00:48:23.564 --> 00:48:24.533
find out how to medicate it.
I don't have enough

1091
00:48:24.533 --> 00:48:27.370
seratonin in my brain so I better take something.

1092
00:48:27.370 --> 00:48:29.582
I think that's a great mistake.

1093
00:48:29.582 --> 00:48:32.861
We're not supposed to be happy all the time.

1094
00:48:32.861 --> 00:48:37.861
So beyond breath, what are other practices?

1095
00:48:37.910 --> 00:48:38.743
I'm sure there are

1096
00:48:38.743 --> 00:48:39.576
certain things--
It's all the things

1097
00:48:39.576 --> 00:48:40.920
you know, it's like physical activity

1098
00:48:40.920 --> 00:48:45.650
is one of the most powerful ways of stabilizing mood.

1099
00:48:45.650 --> 00:48:49.806
Nutrition has an enormous effect on brain function mood.

1100
00:48:49.806 --> 00:48:53.671
I think who you associate with the moods are contagious.

1101
00:48:53.671 --> 00:48:56.390
You know, if you spend time in the company

1102
00:48:56.390 --> 00:48:57.398
of people who are depressed and anxious,

1103
00:48:57.398 --> 00:48:59.511
you're more likely to be depressed and anxious.

1104
00:48:59.511 --> 00:49:01.490
I mean, there's actual research showing that

1105
00:49:01.490 --> 00:49:02.990
if you have a happy friend

1106
00:49:02.990 --> 00:49:04.700
who lives within a half mile of you,

1107
00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:06.093
you are more likely to be happy

1108
00:49:06.093 --> 00:49:07.950
and the effect falls off with distance.

1109
00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:10.283
You can actually measure this.

1110
00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:15.470
I mean that's something I tell people to do.

1111
00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:18.210
But only people have never think about that.

1112
00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:22.141
Well, a big piece of that is this loss of community

1113
00:49:22.141 --> 00:49:25.380
that I think is endemic to modern culture.

1114
00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:27.764
And especially acute right now during the pandemic.

1115
00:49:27.764 --> 00:49:31.684
Of course, I'm sure are you friends with Dan Buettner?

1116
00:49:31.684 --> 00:49:32.517
Yeah.

1117
00:49:32.517 --> 00:49:33.350
You know, Dan?

1118
00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:34.183
Yeah.

1119
00:49:34.183 --> 00:49:35.090
Dan's coming over here tomorrow.

1120
00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:36.360
Oh great tell him hi.

1121
00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:38.484
But you know, one of the many things I appreciate

1122
00:49:38.484 --> 00:49:42.214
about his work is that he understands

1123
00:49:42.214 --> 00:49:44.165
that you can't shoulder the individual

1124
00:49:44.165 --> 00:49:48.432
with trying to solve all these problems on their own.

1125
00:49:48.432 --> 00:49:52.015
We have to re-imagine how we construct communities

1126
00:49:52.015 --> 00:49:56.194
to make these things just in the background.

1127
00:49:56.194 --> 00:49:58.013
So that they're the easy choices.

1128
00:49:58.013 --> 00:50:02.111
Whether it's bike lanes or access to healthy food or,

1129
00:50:02.111 --> 00:50:05.614
neighborhoods organized, so that community is inevitable.

1130
00:50:05.614 --> 00:50:08.802
Very good point, and I think--

1131
00:50:08.802 --> 00:50:12.610
And that's part and parcel of the integrative medicine

1132
00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:13.510
message, right?
Yes.

1133
00:50:13.510 --> 00:50:16.396
Like those two worlds should merge with each other

1134
00:50:16.396 --> 00:50:17.436
in some way.

1135
00:50:17.436 --> 00:50:20.570
We place a great deal of emphasis on community

1136
00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:23.089
and the importance of community to healing and health.

1137
00:50:23.089 --> 00:50:24.303
Absolutely.

1138
00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:30.800
Another thing that most strike you as kind of amazing is

1139
00:50:32.267 --> 00:50:36.039
the kind of recent appreciation for mushrooms.

1140
00:50:36.039 --> 00:50:37.180
(Andrew laughs)

1141
00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:39.423
Non-psychoactive mushrooms.

1142
00:50:39.423 --> 00:50:42.010
It's like everyone's drinking mushroom coffee

1143
00:50:42.010 --> 00:50:44.226
and we're all talking about healthy mushrooms.

1144
00:50:44.226 --> 00:50:46.139
And this is something that

1145
00:50:46.139 --> 00:50:48.730
you've been talking about forever.

1146
00:50:48.730 --> 00:50:51.140
I was fascinated from mushrooms from an early age

1147
00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:53.373
I think partly because my mother was very afraid of them.

1148
00:50:53.373 --> 00:50:57.194
And she told me when mushrooms came up on our lawn

1149
00:50:57.194 --> 00:50:59.110
that I shouldn't even touch them

1150
00:50:59.110 --> 00:51:00.560
because they either got poisoned.

1151
00:51:00.560 --> 00:51:03.230
And somehow that made me wanna know about them.

1152
00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:05.550
They seem strange and interesting.

1153
00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:06.580
And actually throughout history

1154
00:51:06.580 --> 00:51:08.656
I think people have found mushrooms strange.

1155
00:51:08.656 --> 00:51:10.593
No, that it's unclear.

1156
00:51:10.593 --> 00:51:13.000
They didn't grow from roots like plants.

1157
00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:15.260
And they would suddenly appear from one day to the next,

1158
00:51:15.260 --> 00:51:19.112
their strange colors and odors and tastes,

1159
00:51:19.112 --> 00:51:21.009
and some of them are utterly delicious

1160
00:51:21.009 --> 00:51:22.580
and there's some that can kill you.

1161
00:51:22.580 --> 00:51:24.224
And now we found all of these

1162
00:51:24.224 --> 00:51:26.173
that have interesting medicinal effects.

1163
00:51:26.173 --> 00:51:29.937
They're really unusual, interesting life forms.

1164
00:51:29.937 --> 00:51:33.368
And as you may have heard we are more closely related

1165
00:51:33.368 --> 00:51:35.026
to mushrooms than we're to plants.

1166
00:51:35.026 --> 00:51:36.163
Yeah, that's a mindblower.

1167
00:51:36.163 --> 00:51:40.520
And the extent to which the underground invisible network

1168
00:51:40.520 --> 00:51:44.658
is a communication network that basically plays

1169
00:51:44.658 --> 00:51:48.071
this massive role in the entire eco system.

1170
00:51:48.071 --> 00:51:51.280
Exactly, I mean, I remember learning this must've been

1171
00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:54.960
in the seventies or eighties, and I had no idea this

1172
00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:57.410
that trees in the forest could not live

1173
00:51:57.410 --> 00:52:01.073
unless they form symbiotic associations with mushrooms.

1174
00:52:01.073 --> 00:52:04.829
That the rootlets of trees actually become sheathed

1175
00:52:04.829 --> 00:52:07.305
with the mycelium of mushrooms.

1176
00:52:07.305 --> 00:52:09.376
And if you take tree seedlings

1177
00:52:09.376 --> 00:52:12.345
and sterilize the roots and plant them

1178
00:52:12.345 --> 00:52:15.943
the trees are completely stunted and don't grow normally.

1179
00:52:15.943 --> 00:52:18.043
I mean, I had studied biology at Harvard

1180
00:52:18.043 --> 00:52:18.970
I never heard of that,

1181
00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:20.699
it's a completely new idea.
Yeah that's wild.

1182
00:52:20.699 --> 00:52:24.140
And now Paul Stamets is the guy

1183
00:52:24.140 --> 00:52:25.860
that everyone wants to talk to about this,

1184
00:52:25.860 --> 00:52:28.111
but you're the guy who introduced him to this whole thing,

1185
00:52:28.111 --> 00:52:29.010
which is crazy.

1186
00:52:29.010 --> 00:52:32.338
I read that in Michael Pollan's book.

1187
00:52:32.338 --> 00:52:34.527
I got him interested especially in medicinal mushrooms.

1188
00:52:34.527 --> 00:52:37.290
'Cause he was only interested in the magic ones

1189
00:52:37.290 --> 00:52:39.773
when I met him when he was a young guy.

1190
00:52:40.790 --> 00:52:44.000
So of all the mushrooms, like what are the ones that

1191
00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:46.860
you feel like we should be incorporating into our routine?

1192
00:52:46.860 --> 00:52:49.935
Oh gosh, I think it's probably best to incorporate

1193
00:52:49.935 --> 00:52:53.550
several, a number of different species at the same time

1194
00:52:53.550 --> 00:52:56.797
because they seem to work synergistically.

1195
00:52:56.797 --> 00:53:01.797
I think reishi, which has good anti-inflammatory effect.

1196
00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:06.373
Maitake mushrooms, oyster mushroom, shiitake.

1197
00:53:08.003 --> 00:53:11.530
A lot of these are the ones that have immune modulating

1198
00:53:11.530 --> 00:53:14.487
effects that increase our resistance to infection

1199
00:53:14.487 --> 00:53:17.010
and cancer, very useful.

1200
00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:20.164
Lion's mane, which is now being cultivated.

1201
00:53:20.164 --> 00:53:22.076
Why it's a very good edible mushroom.

1202
00:53:22.076 --> 00:53:25.993
But this has a unique nerve growth factor.

1203
00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:28.322
Appears to improve cognitive function,

1204
00:53:28.322 --> 00:53:30.890
may help ward off dementia.

1205
00:53:30.890 --> 00:53:33.295
I think that's a really good one to learn about.

1206
00:53:33.295 --> 00:53:36.190
Chaga which is one another one of these

1207
00:53:36.190 --> 00:53:39.181
shelf fungi that grow in trees.

1208
00:53:39.181 --> 00:53:41.910
And how about cordyceps?

1209
00:53:41.910 --> 00:53:42.743
Is that --
That's my favorite.

1210
00:53:42.743 --> 00:53:43.823
Okay I thought it could be.

1211
00:53:43.823 --> 00:53:46.600
I mean, it works as an endurance booster

1212
00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:50.355
in terms of how it helps you sort of maximize oxygen uptake.

1213
00:53:50.355 --> 00:53:51.932
Like it's very effective.

1214
00:53:51.932 --> 00:53:54.150
So this is a strange one, it's a parasitic--

1215
00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:55.537
(words lost in cross talk)

1216
00:53:55.537 --> 00:53:56.720
Yes it's pretty gross.

1217
00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:59.535
It's a fungus that grows on the back of a caterpillar.

1218
00:53:59.535 --> 00:54:00.790
Caterpillar right.

1219
00:54:00.790 --> 00:54:02.060
At very high altitudes.

1220
00:54:02.060 --> 00:54:03.720
Yeah and that kills the caterpillar

1221
00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:05.810
and then sends up this fruiting stock.

1222
00:54:05.810 --> 00:54:08.786
Although in practice now it's cultivated on grain.

1223
00:54:08.786 --> 00:54:11.939
But this has a long history of use in

1224
00:54:11.939 --> 00:54:16.692
traditional Chinese medicine for people who are debilitated,

1225
00:54:16.692 --> 00:54:19.630
either from injury or illness

1226
00:54:20.480 --> 00:54:22.394
or old age of increasing energy.

1227
00:54:22.394 --> 00:54:24.780
And there's been good studies showing increases

1228
00:54:24.780 --> 00:54:26.006
aerobic capacity.

1229
00:54:26.006 --> 00:54:28.454
So that's a good tasting mushroom.

1230
00:54:28.454 --> 00:54:30.927
And now it's a good one to know about.

1231
00:54:30.927 --> 00:54:33.500
The more you learn about this

1232
00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:35.570
you realize just how mystical and magical--

1233
00:54:35.570 --> 00:54:36.550
(words lost in cross talk)

1234
00:54:36.550 --> 00:54:37.570
It's insane.

1235
00:54:37.570 --> 00:54:40.040
I love mystical and magical.

1236
00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:41.480
Good to keep that in mind.
That's your thing now right?

1237
00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:46.480
Where does the interest in matcha come in?

1238
00:54:47.507 --> 00:54:49.313
Is that when you traveled to Japan?

1239
00:54:49.313 --> 00:54:51.692
I went to Japan first, when I was 17.

1240
00:54:51.692 --> 00:54:54.097
Part of that when I was in that

1241
00:54:54.097 --> 00:54:56.914
round the world student exchange program.

1242
00:54:56.914 --> 00:54:59.113
And I lived with Japanese families.

1243
00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:01.818
First one was outside of Tokyo.

1244
00:55:01.818 --> 00:55:04.925
This was 1959 when Japan was a very different place.

1245
00:55:04.925 --> 00:55:07.218
And the family I live with,

1246
00:55:07.218 --> 00:55:09.430
there were supposed to be a student there

1247
00:55:09.430 --> 00:55:10.700
that's that was studying English

1248
00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:12.474
but we had no language in common.

1249
00:55:12.474 --> 00:55:15.403
But the second night I was there, the mother of the family

1250
00:55:15.403 --> 00:55:18.810
indicated she wanted to take me next door to her neighbor

1251
00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:20.318
who was a practitioner of tea ceremony.

1252
00:55:20.318 --> 00:55:22.383
And so the three of us sat around

1253
00:55:22.383 --> 00:55:24.287
and this woman made matcha.

1254
00:55:24.287 --> 00:55:25.120
And first of all,

1255
00:55:25.120 --> 00:55:28.010
I was blown away by the color of matcha.

1256
00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:31.355
I'd never seen that vibrant green was just amazing.

1257
00:55:31.355 --> 00:55:33.762
And then also the bamboo whisk

1258
00:55:33.762 --> 00:55:36.225
that's used to whisk matcha in a bowl.

1259
00:55:36.225 --> 00:55:39.143
It's just a marvel of Japanese craftsmanship.

1260
00:55:39.143 --> 00:55:40.530
I was fascinated with.

1261
00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:45.530
So in the 1970s, I was going to Japan fairly regularly.

1262
00:55:47.272 --> 00:55:49.470
And whenever I'd go, I'd bring Matcha back

1263
00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:50.730
and turn people onto it.

1264
00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:53.010
Nobody that I knew had ever experienced

1265
00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:54.260
or knew anything about it.

1266
00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:58.034
And somewhere I think it was in the 19, late 1980s, 1990s,

1267
00:55:58.034 --> 00:56:00.782
I tried to sell matcha through my website.

1268
00:56:00.782 --> 00:56:02.070
I imported it.

1269
00:56:02.070 --> 00:56:04.226
I found a company that I could get it from in Japan

1270
00:56:04.226 --> 00:56:06.038
but it was way ahead of its time.

1271
00:56:06.038 --> 00:56:08.580
So people weren't ready for it.

1272
00:56:08.580 --> 00:56:12.415
And then, I was amazed to see matcha becoming popular.

1273
00:56:12.415 --> 00:56:14.278
Just one of the many things--

1274
00:56:14.278 --> 00:56:15.280
Just one of the many things

1275
00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:16.670
I was way ahead of.
(words lost in cross talk)

1276
00:56:16.670 --> 00:56:18.830
However, I very disappointed that

1277
00:56:18.830 --> 00:56:22.494
the matcha that I saw here was really inferior

1278
00:56:22.494 --> 00:56:27.140
because matcha it's such a fine powder

1279
00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:28.663
that it oxidizes really quickly.

1280
00:56:28.663 --> 00:56:33.070
And when it oxidizes, it loses that bright green color.

1281
00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:33.903
It becomes bitter.

1282
00:56:33.903 --> 00:56:35.152
It's not pleasant.

1283
00:56:35.152 --> 00:56:38.020
And most of what I saw available here was that way.

1284
00:56:38.020 --> 00:56:39.780
So I was determined to see

1285
00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:42.083
if I could make really good matcha available.

1286
00:56:42.083 --> 00:56:45.470
So I found that a company that managed to get

1287
00:56:45.470 --> 00:56:48.330
the URL matcha.com which is a big score.

1288
00:56:48.330 --> 00:56:49.163
Yeah that is.

1289
00:56:49.163 --> 00:56:50.803
And made a connection with a traditional

1290
00:56:50.803 --> 00:56:52.522
matcha producer in Japan.

1291
00:56:52.522 --> 00:56:54.810
And we import and sell

1292
00:56:54.810 --> 00:56:56.531
I think some of the best matcha you can get.

1293
00:56:56.531 --> 00:57:01.225
And by the way we can offer your listeners a discount.

1294
00:57:01.225 --> 00:57:02.135
Oh cool.

1295
00:57:02.135 --> 00:57:05.400
So it's matcha.com, the company is Matcha Kari.

1296
00:57:05.400 --> 00:57:07.836
And if they use the discount code rich 15.

1297
00:57:07.836 --> 00:57:08.669
All right.

1298
00:57:08.669 --> 00:57:09.502
They will get a discount--

1299
00:57:09.502 --> 00:57:10.335
(words lost in cross talk)

1300
00:57:10.335 --> 00:57:11.168
I think that's very generous thank you.

1301
00:57:11.168 --> 00:57:14.250
But matcha, it's the only form of tea

1302
00:57:14.250 --> 00:57:16.132
that in which you consume the whole leaf

1303
00:57:16.132 --> 00:57:19.090
and it's got a higher content of antioxidants

1304
00:57:19.090 --> 00:57:23.350
and flavor compounds and other helpful compounds.

1305
00:57:23.350 --> 00:57:24.257
And some of this is because

1306
00:57:24.257 --> 00:57:26.946
of the unusual way that the leaves are grown

1307
00:57:26.946 --> 00:57:29.359
in the last three weeks before harvest,

1308
00:57:29.359 --> 00:57:31.961
they're grown under a very heavy shade cloth,

1309
00:57:31.961 --> 00:57:36.304
90% shade cloth and then response to that absence of light

1310
00:57:36.304 --> 00:57:38.440
the leaves grow bigger

1311
00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:40.290
and thinner and produce more chlorophyll

1312
00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:41.920
which is why it's so bright green

1313
00:57:41.920 --> 00:57:44.746
and more L-theanine which is the calming compound

1314
00:57:44.746 --> 00:57:46.700
that moderates the caffeine

1315
00:57:46.700 --> 00:57:48.911
and antioxidants and other good things.

1316
00:57:48.911 --> 00:57:51.250
So I think for all those reasons matcha

1317
00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:54.585
is one of the most helpful forms of tea out there.

1318
00:57:54.585 --> 00:57:55.536
I love it.

1319
00:57:55.536 --> 00:57:58.360
It does give you a little bit of a boost,

1320
00:57:58.360 --> 00:57:59.629
but it's kind of a calm energy.

1321
00:57:59.629 --> 00:58:00.462
Exactly.

1322
00:58:00.462 --> 00:58:01.295
And sustains itself for a little bit longer.

1323
00:58:01.295 --> 00:58:02.208
Very different from coffee.

1324
00:58:02.208 --> 00:58:04.350
And the color is magical.

1325
00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:05.860
It's magical. I love it.

1326
00:58:05.860 --> 00:58:06.855
It tastes great.

1327
00:58:06.855 --> 00:58:11.855
But I know well, the difference between a fine matcha

1328
00:58:12.115 --> 00:58:15.287
and what you typically see, it's a much darker color.

1329
00:58:15.287 --> 00:58:16.531
Darker or yellowish.

1330
00:58:16.531 --> 00:58:18.749
Yeah, you can tell it's been oxidized.

1331
00:58:18.749 --> 00:58:20.665
And I see this with a lot of these,

1332
00:58:20.665 --> 00:58:23.630
quote unquote superfood products,

1333
00:58:23.630 --> 00:58:26.340
the more money there is to be made

1334
00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:30.215
with the lack of kind of quality control or regulation

1335
00:58:30.215 --> 00:58:32.386
in this space,

1336
00:58:32.386 --> 00:58:35.283
whether it's Moringa or, whatever it is,

1337
00:58:35.283 --> 00:58:37.600
Camu camu, whatever you're trying to get.

1338
00:58:37.600 --> 00:58:39.370
It's really hard to know

1339
00:58:39.370 --> 00:58:41.204
like what's the good stuff and what's--

1340
00:58:41.204 --> 00:58:42.051
(indistinct)
Yeah and if you've had

1341
00:58:42.051 --> 00:58:44.403
no experience of the good stuff, you don't know.

1342
00:58:44.403 --> 00:58:47.145
Right and you don't know how it's harvested,

1343
00:58:47.145 --> 00:58:48.750
how it's grown, how it's picked,

1344
00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:50.140
how it's stored, how it's shipped,

1345
00:58:50.140 --> 00:58:51.844
all of those things play into

1346
00:58:51.844 --> 00:58:54.632
whether or not it's maintaining the qualities

1347
00:58:54.632 --> 00:58:57.890
that you're purchasing it for.

1348
00:58:57.890 --> 00:59:01.010
And most of these products are not good for that reason.

1349
00:59:01.010 --> 00:59:01.843
Exactly.

1350
00:59:01.843 --> 00:59:03.345
By the way, I get a little disillusioned

1351
00:59:03.345 --> 00:59:05.155
with all the talk about superfoods

1352
00:59:05.155 --> 00:59:08.703
because we have plenty of superfoods right here,

1353
00:59:08.703 --> 00:59:11.150
like berries are superfoods

1354
00:59:11.150 --> 00:59:13.633
and you can get very good quality organic

1355
00:59:13.633 --> 00:59:16.770
berries that are just loaded with antioxidants

1356
00:59:16.770 --> 00:59:18.541
and anticancer compounds.

1357
00:59:18.541 --> 00:59:20.641
Now that those are superfoods.

1358
00:59:20.641 --> 00:59:23.164
Going all the way back to the botany days.

1359
00:59:23.164 --> 00:59:24.180
(Andrew laughs)

1360
00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:25.013
Yeah exactly.

1361
00:59:25.013 --> 00:59:26.533
How many people were in the botany department.

1362
00:59:26.533 --> 00:59:29.488
It was an attic of the university, you know, first of all,

1363
00:59:29.488 --> 00:59:32.600
it was the botanical museum.
You're studying what?

1364
00:59:34.420 --> 00:59:36.918
Biology was being completely taken over by

1365
00:59:36.918 --> 00:59:39.881
molecular biology and gene stuff at that point.

1366
00:59:39.881 --> 00:59:43.260
And botany was a little annex

1367
00:59:43.260 --> 00:59:46.074
that was considered so antiquated.

1368
00:59:46.074 --> 00:59:48.612
And it was in the botanical museum

1369
00:59:48.612 --> 00:59:51.670
which was above the glass flowers in the--

1370
00:59:51.670 --> 00:59:54.160
In the Gregor Mendel wing or something.

1371
00:59:54.160 --> 00:59:55.490
Exactly right.

1372
00:59:55.490 --> 00:59:57.179
But my professor

1373
00:59:57.179 --> 01:00:00.985
Dick Schultes was the father of modern ethnobotany.

1374
01:00:00.985 --> 01:00:03.180
And that was just a wonderful association

1375
01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:04.501
that I formed with him.

1376
01:00:04.501 --> 01:00:07.778
And having that background in botany

1377
01:00:07.778 --> 01:00:09.919
has been invaluable to me.

1378
01:00:09.919 --> 01:00:13.480
I know very few physicians who studied botany

1379
01:00:14.360 --> 01:00:18.450
and it was a shock to me when I went to medical school

1380
01:00:18.450 --> 01:00:20.509
to see that the people teaching me pharmacology

1381
01:00:20.509 --> 01:00:23.440
generally had no firsthand knowledge

1382
01:00:23.440 --> 01:00:26.380
of the plants that these drugs came from.

1383
01:00:26.380 --> 01:00:29.120
And to see how different the whole plants were

1384
01:00:29.120 --> 01:00:31.250
from the chemicals isolated from them.

1385
01:00:31.250 --> 01:00:33.008
So that's given me a great perspective.

1386
01:00:33.008 --> 01:00:35.980
Well, I mean, no surprise given that

1387
01:00:35.980 --> 01:00:39.643
the idea that nutrition in and of itself really had no value

1388
01:00:39.643 --> 01:00:43.306
in terms of how the organism operates.

1389
01:00:43.306 --> 01:00:45.868
So, but that's changing.

1390
01:00:45.868 --> 01:00:49.518
I love all the new sciences coming out about the microbiome.

1391
01:00:49.518 --> 01:00:51.200
That's fantastic.

1392
01:00:51.200 --> 01:00:52.583
That is such a revolution.

1393
01:00:52.583 --> 01:00:55.510
That's as fascinating as that mushroom kingdom.

1394
01:00:55.510 --> 01:00:56.500
Absolutely.

1395
01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:58.100
And is mysterious I think.

1396
01:00:58.100 --> 01:00:59.533
Yeah, when I was in medical school,

1397
01:00:59.533 --> 01:01:03.000
the teaching was that yes there were these organisms

1398
01:01:03.000 --> 01:01:05.270
in the colon and they helped with digestion,

1399
01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:06.103
but that was it.

1400
01:01:06.103 --> 01:01:08.318
And that people who took

1401
01:01:08.318 --> 01:01:11.625
acidophilus or ate yogurt were health not so.

1402
01:01:11.625 --> 01:01:13.930
Boy has that changed.

1403
01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:15.029
Yeah. It's changed a lot.

1404
01:01:15.029 --> 01:01:19.227
Well, I'm a long time plant-based vegan person.

1405
01:01:19.227 --> 01:01:20.291
I know you're not vegan,

1406
01:01:20.291 --> 01:01:23.364
but somebody who appreciates the plant kingdom,

1407
01:01:23.364 --> 01:01:26.113
perhaps as much as anybody.

1408
01:01:27.300 --> 01:01:28.990
So I love talking about plants.

1409
01:01:28.990 --> 01:01:31.040
Can I just say also I love talking about plants.

1410
01:01:31.040 --> 01:01:32.168
I love plants. I garden a lot.

1411
01:01:32.168 --> 01:01:34.850
I have lots of plants around me all the time,

1412
01:01:34.850 --> 01:01:37.766
but one of the reasons that I would love to see

1413
01:01:37.766 --> 01:01:40.768
people move in the direction of eating more plants

1414
01:01:40.768 --> 01:01:43.044
and reduce consumption of animals.

1415
01:01:43.044 --> 01:01:46.978
This is a very concrete thing that we could do

1416
01:01:46.978 --> 01:01:51.264
to deal with one of the factors that's driving these

1417
01:01:51.264 --> 01:01:55.021
zoonotic diseases that are responsible for this pandemic

1418
01:01:55.021 --> 01:01:59.070
and which are putting us at great risk for future pandemics

1419
01:01:59.070 --> 01:01:59.981
of very serious diseases.

1420
01:01:59.981 --> 01:02:03.485
It's that we are encouraging organisms to jump

1421
01:02:03.485 --> 01:02:07.090
from animals to people because of climate change,

1422
01:02:07.090 --> 01:02:10.170
deforestation, that our methods of agriculture

1423
01:02:10.170 --> 01:02:11.038
and you look at the ones,

1424
01:02:11.038 --> 01:02:12.870
what are we gonna do something about

1425
01:02:12.870 --> 01:02:14.747
one of them very concretely is it to

1426
01:02:14.747 --> 01:02:16.970
reduce our dependence on animals for food.

1427
01:02:16.970 --> 01:02:20.940
And if there was just less of that, that would put us

1428
01:02:20.940 --> 01:02:22.330
ahead of the game.

1429
01:02:22.330 --> 01:02:23.260
Yeah, a hundred percent

1430
01:02:23.260 --> 01:02:26.090
in the way that we need to appreciate the human body

1431
01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:27.945
and its ability to heal and self regulate.

1432
01:02:27.945 --> 01:02:30.168
We need to own that perspective

1433
01:02:30.168 --> 01:02:31.988
with respect to the planet, right?

1434
01:02:31.988 --> 01:02:32.821
For sure.

1435
01:02:32.821 --> 01:02:34.523
And if we're,

1436
01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:37.330
clearing rainforests

1437
01:02:37.330 --> 01:02:41.730
and creating algal blooms in our waterways

1438
01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:43.790
we're devastating ecosystems,

1439
01:02:43.790 --> 01:02:45.526
and we're creating less and less

1440
01:02:45.526 --> 01:02:48.417
areas for these species to exist

1441
01:02:48.417 --> 01:02:51.247
in the manner in which, they're meant to exist.

1442
01:02:51.247 --> 01:02:54.394
And so they end up encroaching on other areas

1443
01:02:54.394 --> 01:02:56.950
and these species are coming into contact

1444
01:02:56.950 --> 01:02:58.420
that perhaps wouldn't otherwise,

1445
01:02:58.420 --> 01:03:01.580
and all of this creates this toxic situation

1446
01:03:01.580 --> 01:03:05.660
that leads to zoonotic disease and the decay of the planet

1447
01:03:05.660 --> 01:03:07.496
and a very simple, practical

1448
01:03:07.496 --> 01:03:10.963
and effective thing that we can all do as individuals

1449
01:03:10.963 --> 01:03:13.796
is reduce that meat intake.

1450
01:03:13.796 --> 01:03:15.318
You still like the fish though.

1451
01:03:15.318 --> 01:03:16.993
I still like fish.

1452
01:03:19.370 --> 01:03:22.556
I became a lacto-vegetarian in 1970.

1453
01:03:22.556 --> 01:03:27.150
And I ate that way for about 15 years.

1454
01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:29.940
And then I was going to Japan frequently.

1455
01:03:29.940 --> 01:03:32.720
It is very difficult to go to Japan and not eat fish

1456
01:03:32.720 --> 01:03:34.267
because fish is in everything.

1457
01:03:34.267 --> 01:03:37.190
And then I was reading about health benefits of fish.

1458
01:03:37.190 --> 01:03:38.960
So I started eating fish.

1459
01:03:38.960 --> 01:03:42.470
And since then I've been a pesce, pescatarian.

1460
01:03:42.470 --> 01:03:44.708
But you're not going to Japan right now.

1461
01:03:44.708 --> 01:03:45.831
That's correct.

1462
01:03:45.831 --> 01:03:46.680
(laughs)

1463
01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:49.297
Come on, I know Sylvia Earle was all over you.

1464
01:03:49.297 --> 01:03:50.310
She was all over me.

1465
01:03:50.310 --> 01:03:52.747
She begged me to tell people not to eat fish.

1466
01:03:52.747 --> 01:03:56.264
Did you watch, "Seaspiracy" on Netflix?

1467
01:03:56.264 --> 01:03:57.300
Yeah.

1468
01:03:57.300 --> 01:03:58.720
She's great in that movie.

1469
01:03:58.720 --> 01:03:59.553
She's great.

1470
01:03:59.553 --> 01:04:01.590
It's a big problem where there aren't

1471
01:04:01.590 --> 01:04:02.815
gonna be fish in the ocean.

1472
01:04:02.815 --> 01:04:05.079
From the way we've over harvested them.

1473
01:04:05.079 --> 01:04:07.480
I'm gonna convert you back to being vegan.

1474
01:04:07.480 --> 01:04:08.633
All right you can work on it.

1475
01:04:08.633 --> 01:04:11.110
How do you feel about lab grown meat

1476
01:04:11.110 --> 01:04:11.943
or lab grown (indistinct)?

1477
01:04:11.943 --> 01:04:13.383
I think It's interesting,

1478
01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:19.351
I mean look we can have a discussion

1479
01:04:19.351 --> 01:04:22.645
on the margins about the health implications of that

1480
01:04:22.645 --> 01:04:27.645
but I think we need to focus on the elephant in the room

1481
01:04:27.960 --> 01:04:30.305
which is the extent to which our dependence

1482
01:04:30.305 --> 01:04:34.100
on industrialized animal agriculture is wreaking havoc

1483
01:04:34.100 --> 01:04:34.933
on the planet.

1484
01:04:34.933 --> 01:04:38.488
We are a meat obsessed world, and that has to change

1485
01:04:38.488 --> 01:04:40.409
if we wanna preserve our ecosystems.

1486
01:04:40.409 --> 01:04:45.409
This is one tool in a tool kit for accomplishing that.

1487
01:04:46.700 --> 01:04:47.533
I agree.

1488
01:04:47.533 --> 01:04:49.090
And I would say probably, I would tell people

1489
01:04:49.090 --> 01:04:51.029
in this country, the place to start is with beef

1490
01:04:51.029 --> 01:04:52.590
because I think that's the one

1491
01:04:52.590 --> 01:04:55.040
that has the worst consequences.

1492
01:04:55.040 --> 01:05:00.040
Yeah there's also issues of antibiotic resistance

1493
01:05:01.578 --> 01:05:06.578
it's a toxic stew of so many problems that we could redress

1494
01:05:07.168 --> 01:05:09.732
if we just reduce that dependency on it.

1495
01:05:09.732 --> 01:05:13.390
So whether you go a hundred percent plant-based

1496
01:05:13.390 --> 01:05:15.994
or 90% plant-based, or you start,

1497
01:05:15.994 --> 01:05:18.684
just making some simple changes

1498
01:05:18.684 --> 01:05:20.220
in your diet on a daily basis,

1499
01:05:20.220 --> 01:05:23.350
like these these things are important to do

1500
01:05:23.350 --> 01:05:24.863
and to take seriously I think.

1501
01:05:24.863 --> 01:05:28.522
So you still like a little cheese though, right?

1502
01:05:28.522 --> 01:05:29.674
(Andrew laughs)

1503
01:05:29.674 --> 01:05:31.719
You know what I did?

1504
01:05:31.719 --> 01:05:35.729
My wife is a plant-based cheese wizard

1505
01:05:35.729 --> 01:05:38.100
and she started this company called SriMu

1506
01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:41.620
she's really like come up with the next evolution of cheese.

1507
01:05:41.620 --> 01:05:43.003
So I brought you some samples I'll share

1508
01:05:43.003 --> 01:05:43.976
with you afterwards.
I would love to try that.

1509
01:05:43.976 --> 01:05:45.267
I will be very interested to do that.

1510
01:05:45.267 --> 01:05:48.910
I'm also very interested in a new micro protein foods

1511
01:05:48.910 --> 01:05:52.737
that are being developed from cultivating fungi

1512
01:05:53.995 --> 01:05:55.841
and mushrooms.

1513
01:05:55.841 --> 01:05:59.180
Yeah. There's this weird intersection

1514
01:06:00.090 --> 01:06:02.534
of the science catching up with

1515
01:06:02.534 --> 01:06:05.680
like sort of hard science catching up with food science

1516
01:06:06.530 --> 01:06:09.952
to create interesting products

1517
01:06:09.952 --> 01:06:13.190
out of everything from algae to mushrooms

1518
01:06:13.190 --> 01:06:14.400
and all this kind of thing.

1519
01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:15.913
Are your kids vegan?

1520
01:06:15.913 --> 01:06:19.242
They are, but they're now at the age where

1521
01:06:19.242 --> 01:06:21.370
they need to have their own exploration.

1522
01:06:21.370 --> 01:06:24.000
So we give them a wide leash.

1523
01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:28.337
I mean, they stray from the hardcore vegan path,

1524
01:06:28.337 --> 01:06:31.537
but they've never had meat, so they'll have,

1525
01:06:31.537 --> 01:06:33.920
but they'll like, you know, my teenage daughter

1526
01:06:33.920 --> 01:06:35.690
she wants to have a beyond burger,

1527
01:06:35.690 --> 01:06:37.313
or stuff like that, you know.

1528
01:06:37.313 --> 01:06:40.510
She wants to have some junk food, you know, it's okay.

1529
01:06:40.510 --> 01:06:43.392
It's like, I'm less interested in that than I am in,

1530
01:06:43.392 --> 01:06:48.279
where her or all of my kids' diet

1531
01:06:48.279 --> 01:06:50.387
is gonna be five, 10 years from now.

1532
01:06:50.387 --> 01:06:52.149
And we've just tried to instill in them,

1533
01:06:52.149 --> 01:06:54.000
a certain level of education.

1534
01:06:54.000 --> 01:06:56.730
So it's less about like the day to day

1535
01:06:56.730 --> 01:07:00.300
and more about like in general, kind of where they fall.

1536
01:07:00.300 --> 01:07:02.905
But it's tricky with people with food.

1537
01:07:02.905 --> 01:07:03.770
I know.

1538
01:07:03.770 --> 01:07:05.980
You know, you've been doing this for a long time.

1539
01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:07.754
Trying to get people to change their habits.

1540
01:07:07.754 --> 01:07:10.575
I mean, come on. Is there anything harder than that?

1541
01:07:10.575 --> 01:07:13.058
How do you approach that when?

1542
01:07:13.058 --> 01:07:15.833
Well I usually tell people not to try to do

1543
01:07:15.833 --> 01:07:19.233
make global change, but to do it in small pieces

1544
01:07:19.233 --> 01:07:22.820
like start by, I mean, the most important thing

1545
01:07:22.820 --> 01:07:24.793
is to start by not eating refined,

1546
01:07:24.793 --> 01:07:27.710
processed and manufactured food.

1547
01:07:27.710 --> 01:07:31.460
That's the main step that I would urge.

1548
01:07:31.460 --> 01:07:33.175
I mean, if we could just do that,

1549
01:07:33.175 --> 01:07:35.020
imagine how different
(words lost in cross talk)

1550
01:07:35.020 --> 01:07:35.853
the world would be.

1551
01:07:35.853 --> 01:07:36.740
And another one is we're

1552
01:07:36.740 --> 01:07:38.700
in such a nutritional mess in this country.

1553
01:07:38.700 --> 01:07:40.670
It's hard to know where to start

1554
01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:42.490
but the other place I'd start is to get people

1555
01:07:42.490 --> 01:07:44.003
to not drink sweet liquids.

1556
01:07:46.136 --> 01:07:49.315
It's not just sodas, it's fruit juice

1557
01:07:49.315 --> 01:07:52.180
and energy drinks and putting sugar in coffee and tea

1558
01:07:52.180 --> 01:07:53.013
and all that.

1559
01:07:53.013 --> 01:07:54.654
That would be a big step.

1560
01:07:54.654 --> 01:07:57.230
And we've got to do something about

1561
01:07:57.230 --> 01:07:59.837
these farm subsidies I think.

1562
01:07:59.837 --> 01:08:02.613
Again, it goes back to a misalignment of incentives.

1563
01:08:02.613 --> 01:08:05.455
Absolutely. Everything is as backward.

1564
01:08:05.455 --> 01:08:09.244
We've made the unhealthiest food cheapest and most available

1565
01:08:09.244 --> 01:08:11.694
and people eat what's cheap and what's available.

1566
01:08:12.638 --> 01:08:15.560
Extending that thought in thinking about

1567
01:08:15.560 --> 01:08:18.511
the disastrous state of healthcare.

1568
01:08:18.511 --> 01:08:21.800
If you had your druthers,

1569
01:08:21.800 --> 01:08:24.446
like how would you address health care reform?

1570
01:08:24.446 --> 01:08:27.680
Well, first of all, we don't have a healthcare system

1571
01:08:27.680 --> 01:08:28.513
in this country.

1572
01:08:28.513 --> 01:08:29.986
We have a disease management system

1573
01:08:29.986 --> 01:08:32.198
that's functioning very imperfectly

1574
01:08:32.198 --> 01:08:33.870
and getting worse by the minute.

1575
01:08:33.870 --> 01:08:36.255
We have to have universal healthcare.

1576
01:08:36.255 --> 01:08:39.010
It's unconscionable that the richest nation

1577
01:08:39.010 --> 01:08:40.950
on earth can't guarantee basic healthcare

1578
01:08:40.950 --> 01:08:42.020
to all of its citizens.

1579
01:08:42.020 --> 01:08:43.020
You're socialist.

1580
01:08:43.020 --> 01:08:44.333
Yeah. Well, there you go.

1581
01:08:44.333 --> 01:08:46.833
I think there should be a universal income to.

1582
01:08:47.879 --> 01:08:50.538
Then I think we have to really break the hold

1583
01:08:50.538 --> 01:08:55.538
of those vested interests on the system.

1584
01:08:55.850 --> 01:09:00.673
I really see, we have to get away from dependence

1585
01:09:00.673 --> 01:09:04.640
on expensive technology for managing conditions

1586
01:09:04.640 --> 01:09:07.696
in terms of shifting the whole enterprise away from

1587
01:09:07.696 --> 01:09:10.280
disease management to health promotion.

1588
01:09:10.280 --> 01:09:12.825
The problem is you run up again against those

1589
01:09:12.825 --> 01:09:14.685
vested interests again.

1590
01:09:14.685 --> 01:09:19.123
It's whether one example is the subs form subsidies.

1591
01:09:20.388 --> 01:09:24.890
There's last I looked something like 46% of US hospitals

1592
01:09:24.890 --> 01:09:27.330
had fast food restaurants in their premises.

1593
01:09:27.330 --> 01:09:28.747
And if you try to change that,

1594
01:09:28.747 --> 01:09:31.041
this brings money to the hospital.

1595
01:09:31.041 --> 01:09:33.670
It's like almost everywhere you look,

1596
01:09:33.670 --> 01:09:35.695
it's like, you're up against that kind of stuff.

1597
01:09:35.695 --> 01:09:38.280
Well, and in the way that the human body

1598
01:09:38.280 --> 01:09:39.884
and the planet are holistic organisms

1599
01:09:39.884 --> 01:09:41.335
politics as a whole like,

1600
01:09:41.335 --> 01:09:43.150
you can't talk about healthcare reform

1601
01:09:43.150 --> 01:09:45.912
without talking about election reform

1602
01:09:45.912 --> 01:09:48.820
and campaign finance reform, because it's the lobbying,

1603
01:09:48.820 --> 01:09:51.259
industrial complex

1604
01:09:51.259 --> 01:09:53.020
(words lost in cross talk)
There has to be

1605
01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:55.048
a grassroots social political movement

1606
01:09:55.048 --> 01:09:57.031
in which we start electing different

1607
01:09:57.031 --> 01:09:58.996
kinds of representatives who weren't beholden

1608
01:09:58.996 --> 01:10:00.560
to those vested interests.

1609
01:10:00.560 --> 01:10:02.160
I don't see that happening.

1610
01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:04.030
Who do you think is getting it right?

1611
01:10:04.030 --> 01:10:05.711
Like overseas, Finland,

1612
01:10:05.711 --> 01:10:06.953
like these other countries.
You know I think

1613
01:10:06.953 --> 01:10:08.520
Germany and Australia do better.

1614
01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:11.300
They both have two tiered systems where there's

1615
01:10:11.300 --> 01:10:14.414
a government insurance and there's also private insurance.

1616
01:10:14.414 --> 01:10:16.860
They seem to work somewhat better.

1617
01:10:16.860 --> 01:10:18.500
But you know, most of the countries,

1618
01:10:18.500 --> 01:10:20.369
you look at the UK, Japan

1619
01:10:20.369 --> 01:10:24.813
that had very well-functioning national health systems.

1620
01:10:24.813 --> 01:10:27.046
They're broken down for the same reasons.

1621
01:10:27.046 --> 01:10:28.474
It's an aging population,

1622
01:10:28.474 --> 01:10:31.567
the epidemics of lifestyle related diseases,

1623
01:10:31.567 --> 01:10:33.455
dependence on expensive technology.

1624
01:10:33.455 --> 01:10:38.185
We're I think farther over the cliff than others.

1625
01:10:38.185 --> 01:10:41.323
I don't see anybody that's got it right.

1626
01:10:43.030 --> 01:10:46.390
When you look at culture right now with the internet,

1627
01:10:46.390 --> 01:10:48.175
it's all about subscription services

1628
01:10:48.175 --> 01:10:51.274
whether it's Netflix or the New York times,

1629
01:10:51.274 --> 01:10:53.629
and I've often thought like healthcare should be,

1630
01:10:53.629 --> 01:10:57.420
like I would subscribe to a service

1631
01:10:57.420 --> 01:10:59.989
that was some hybrid of being able to visit

1632
01:10:59.989 --> 01:11:02.788
practitioners in person and do digital,

1633
01:11:02.788 --> 01:11:04.175
online stuff
That's an interesting idea.

1634
01:11:04.175 --> 01:11:07.045
Where I could get my blood work done.

1635
01:11:07.045 --> 01:11:07.878
Interesting.

1636
01:11:07.878 --> 01:11:10.250
I could consult about diet and nutrition

1637
01:11:10.250 --> 01:11:11.496
and all those things.
Interesting idea.

1638
01:11:11.496 --> 01:11:14.530
And ultimately if develop like,

1639
01:11:14.530 --> 01:11:16.933
oh every week we check in or something like that.

1640
01:11:16.933 --> 01:11:19.632
The insurance company covering that,

1641
01:11:19.632 --> 01:11:22.390
it seems like a leap but they're not gonna then

1642
01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:23.833
have to pay for dialysis or

1643
01:11:23.833 --> 01:11:25.383
(words lost in cross talk)
Well, that's what I mean,

1644
01:11:25.383 --> 01:11:27.630
you got to show them that it's in their interests,

1645
01:11:27.630 --> 01:11:29.805
that you're gonna save them money by this.

1646
01:11:29.805 --> 01:11:32.086
But it's so radical

1647
01:11:32.086 --> 01:11:34.537
compared to the way that we're doing it now and--

1648
01:11:34.537 --> 01:11:35.646
But we're gonna be forced to change

1649
01:11:35.646 --> 01:11:38.050
because this is unsustainable the way it's going.

1650
01:11:38.050 --> 01:11:40.008
Are you optimistic about that?

1651
01:11:40.008 --> 01:11:41.080
(Andrew laughs)

1652
01:11:41.080 --> 01:11:42.686
I think the system's gonna crash.

1653
01:11:42.686 --> 01:11:46.403
We're spending something like 18%

1654
01:11:46.403 --> 01:11:48.880
of our gross domestic product on healthcare

1655
01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:51.070
and we have worst health outcomes

1656
01:11:51.070 --> 01:11:52.970
than any other developed nation.

1657
01:11:52.970 --> 01:11:56.316
It's indefensible in so many ways.

1658
01:11:56.316 --> 01:12:01.153
How do you think about, ancillary to that,

1659
01:12:01.153 --> 01:12:03.737
like the opiate crisis and--

1660
01:12:04.600 --> 01:12:06.130
Well I think that's a great opportunity

1661
01:12:06.130 --> 01:12:06.998
for integrative medicine.

1662
01:12:06.998 --> 01:12:10.499
Because, it's very clear that chronic pain

1663
01:12:10.499 --> 01:12:14.438
can not be managed by opioids alone

1664
01:12:14.438 --> 01:12:16.240
as standalone treatments

1665
01:12:16.240 --> 01:12:18.311
and that treatment has to be integrated.

1666
01:12:18.311 --> 01:12:22.700
So I just see that as an opportunity that's forcing people

1667
01:12:22.700 --> 01:12:25.933
to look at other ways of managing conditions.

1668
01:12:28.090 --> 01:12:32.246
Of all the alternative modalities that you've come across

1669
01:12:32.246 --> 01:12:37.070
hypnosis, meditation, breath work, yoga, all the like.

1670
01:12:37.070 --> 01:12:42.070
What do you think are the most kind of under appreciated?

1671
01:12:42.216 --> 01:12:45.770
Well as I told you, I'm a big fan of osteopathic

1672
01:12:45.770 --> 01:12:47.210
manipulative treatment.

1673
01:12:47.210 --> 01:12:48.100
I think that's great.

1674
01:12:48.100 --> 01:12:50.690
It's unfortunate that only a small percentage

1675
01:12:50.690 --> 01:12:54.236
of osteopaths today do manipulation as a primary therapy.

1676
01:12:54.236 --> 01:12:56.070
And I hope that'll come back

1677
01:12:56.070 --> 01:12:58.083
because it's a brilliant technique.

1678
01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:04.041
As I said, I'm a huge enthusiast for mind body interventions

1679
01:13:04.041 --> 01:13:07.073
and the whole range of them I think are terrific.

1680
01:13:09.810 --> 01:13:14.481
I have long tried to create and boost the field

1681
01:13:14.481 --> 01:13:15.929
of nutritional medicine.

1682
01:13:15.929 --> 01:13:19.119
And when I write treatment plans for patients

1683
01:13:19.119 --> 01:13:22.890
almost always the first item or a couple of items

1684
01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:24.085
were about dietary change

1685
01:13:24.085 --> 01:13:27.714
and making dietary change of primary therapy

1686
01:13:27.714 --> 01:13:30.013
before you even think about other things to do.

1687
01:13:30.013 --> 01:13:33.987
Are there supplements that you recommend

1688
01:13:33.987 --> 01:13:35.973
that you think are appropriate?

1689
01:13:36.950 --> 01:13:38.196
I think probably everybody should

1690
01:13:38.196 --> 01:13:41.319
take a good multi nutrient,

1691
01:13:41.319 --> 01:13:44.185
a supplement that has basic minerals and antioxidants.

1692
01:13:44.185 --> 01:13:47.030
And I think a great public health intervention

1693
01:13:47.030 --> 01:13:49.850
will be to provide a multi nutrient supplement

1694
01:13:49.850 --> 01:13:50.683
free to school kids.

1695
01:13:50.683 --> 01:13:52.440
That's a good idea.

1696
01:13:52.440 --> 01:13:53.273
That seems like a no-brainer.

1697
01:13:53.273 --> 01:13:57.282
And the prison inmates, I think that would do a lot.

1698
01:13:57.282 --> 01:13:58.294
Simple.

1699
01:13:58.294 --> 01:14:00.260
So, and beyond that,

1700
01:14:00.260 --> 01:14:03.250
I think it's probably a good idea for people to take

1701
01:14:03.250 --> 01:14:05.853
vitamin D and check their vitamin D levels.

1702
01:14:06.915 --> 01:14:09.830
I think most people are vitamin D deficient.

1703
01:14:09.830 --> 01:14:11.060
We just live in doors.

1704
01:14:11.060 --> 01:14:13.510
Even in Southern Arizona where I live,

1705
01:14:13.510 --> 01:14:16.250
because dermatologists have made people so paranoid

1706
01:14:16.250 --> 01:14:17.898
of the sun.
Afraid of the sun.

1707
01:14:17.898 --> 01:14:21.670
Well also, it's not just a matter of going outside.

1708
01:14:21.670 --> 01:14:22.798
It depends on where you live.

1709
01:14:22.798 --> 01:14:25.717
Like pretty far south in this time of the year

1710
01:14:25.717 --> 01:14:27.723
for getting vitamin D.

1711
01:14:28.604 --> 01:14:30.628
It's like, the line is through Atlanta.

1712
01:14:30.628 --> 01:14:34.900
So if you live north of Atlanta for half the year

1713
01:14:34.900 --> 01:14:37.750
the sun has a too low on angle for you to make vitamin D.

1714
01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:41.486
So, yeah, I think that would be important.

1715
01:14:41.486 --> 01:14:44.551
How do you think about all of this

1716
01:14:44.551 --> 01:14:49.369
in the context of the pandemic and COVID?

1717
01:14:49.369 --> 01:14:51.730
Well I think the clear principle is that

1718
01:14:51.730 --> 01:14:53.820
the healthy organisms are resistant.

1719
01:14:53.820 --> 01:14:55.510
So the healthier you are,

1720
01:14:55.510 --> 01:14:58.400
the less likely that you are to contract that

1721
01:14:58.400 --> 01:15:00.407
or the less likely to get severely ill.

1722
01:15:00.407 --> 01:15:01.871
How dare you?

1723
01:15:01.871 --> 01:15:04.284
We're not, we're not allowed to talk about that.

1724
01:15:04.284 --> 01:15:05.221
(Andrew laughs)

1725
01:15:05.221 --> 01:15:07.390
That's is dangerous

1726
01:15:07.390 --> 01:15:08.570
But you know, I also have to say

1727
01:15:08.570 --> 01:15:11.830
I think we were so miserably unprepared

1728
01:15:11.830 --> 01:15:13.150
in this country for this.

1729
01:15:13.150 --> 01:15:15.350
And I hope it's a wake up call

1730
01:15:15.350 --> 01:15:17.081
because we're certain to be facing,

1731
01:15:17.081 --> 01:15:19.596
other and probably worse things like this.

1732
01:15:19.596 --> 01:15:21.915
And I hope we get our act together next time.

1733
01:15:21.915 --> 01:15:24.330
Yeah, I hope so. I hope so.

1734
01:15:24.330 --> 01:15:25.680
I mean, listen,

1735
01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:28.270
it's all the things that we've been told to do

1736
01:15:28.270 --> 01:15:30.459
are appropriate from social distancing

1737
01:15:30.459 --> 01:15:32.400
to mask and all of that.

1738
01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:34.613
It's all fine, but it is frustrating

1739
01:15:34.613 --> 01:15:37.069
that there is no conversation about

1740
01:15:37.069 --> 01:15:39.710
taking personal responsibility for your health

1741
01:15:39.710 --> 01:15:43.330
and the idea that we should just be locked down

1742
01:15:43.330 --> 01:15:47.033
and completely isolated and told to stay in doors,

1743
01:15:48.371 --> 01:15:50.662
that doesn't square with what we understand

1744
01:15:50.662 --> 01:15:54.202
about what we need to do to optimize immune function

1745
01:15:54.202 --> 01:15:55.313
of our bodies.

1746
01:15:56.680 --> 01:15:58.789
True. Well, I hope that's gonna change.

1747
01:15:58.789 --> 01:16:00.872
(laughs)

1748
01:16:02.460 --> 01:16:05.170
Final thing I wanted to ask you is

1749
01:16:07.000 --> 01:16:09.589
if in some parallel universe

1750
01:16:09.589 --> 01:16:12.442
you got called up to be surgeon general,

1751
01:16:12.442 --> 01:16:16.963
what would be top of mind to put in place

1752
01:16:16.963 --> 01:16:19.380
in terms of regulations or policy?

1753
01:16:19.380 --> 01:16:21.323
I would really work on nutrition.

1754
01:16:21.323 --> 01:16:24.220
I mean, I think that's such a crying need.

1755
01:16:24.220 --> 01:16:25.797
Remember when Michelle Obama tried to do that though?

1756
01:16:25.797 --> 01:16:26.657
She got crucified.

1757
01:16:26.657 --> 01:16:29.950
I know. But I think that it's gotta be done.

1758
01:16:29.950 --> 01:16:31.600
I mean, whether it's like putting

1759
01:16:32.580 --> 01:16:35.497
a syntax on sugary beverages,

1760
01:16:35.497 --> 01:16:39.939
doing something about fast food restaurants in hospitals

1761
01:16:39.939 --> 01:16:41.450
this agricultural subsidies,

1762
01:16:41.450 --> 01:16:43.650
all that stuff I think is like

1763
01:16:43.650 --> 01:16:45.450
those are critical things that have to change.

1764
01:16:45.450 --> 01:16:47.432
It's crazy that there's still fast food restaurants

1765
01:16:47.432 --> 01:16:48.380
in hospital.

1766
01:16:48.380 --> 01:16:49.213
Unbelievable.

1767
01:16:49.213 --> 01:16:51.289
And how difficult it is to eradicate them.

1768
01:16:51.289 --> 01:16:52.940
I'll tell you one quick story.

1769
01:16:52.940 --> 01:16:55.290
I got a letter a few weeks ago from a first year

1770
01:16:55.290 --> 01:16:57.507
medical student at university of Pennsylvania

1771
01:16:57.507 --> 01:16:59.840
who tried, started to campaign

1772
01:16:59.840 --> 01:17:01.612
to get the McDonald's out of the university hospital.

1773
01:17:01.612 --> 01:17:03.967
He got a petition going among the students.

1774
01:17:03.967 --> 01:17:06.812
They got publicity in the Philadelphia papers

1775
01:17:06.812 --> 01:17:09.450
and he was called in by the Dean of students, who told him

1776
01:17:09.450 --> 01:17:12.099
if he persisted in this, he jeopardizes his medical degree.

1777
01:17:12.099 --> 01:17:14.290
I mean in a nut shell that's the problem.

1778
01:17:14.290 --> 01:17:17.245
There's something very, very wrong with our system.

1779
01:17:17.245 --> 01:17:20.010
That, that would be the case.

1780
01:17:20.010 --> 01:17:21.200
It's terrible, terrible.

1781
01:17:21.200 --> 01:17:23.860
The hospital they'd signed a deal with the devil

1782
01:17:23.860 --> 01:17:25.450
and that was it.

1783
01:17:25.450 --> 01:17:27.780
I know. So this is what we're containing with.

1784
01:17:27.780 --> 01:17:29.573
So we need you to stick around for another 50 years.

1785
01:17:29.573 --> 01:17:30.913
I'll do my best.

1786
01:17:32.680 --> 01:17:34.730
You're a gift.

1787
01:17:34.730 --> 01:17:38.180
I'm so grateful for the work that you've done over the years

1788
01:17:38.180 --> 01:17:39.221
and what you stand for.

1789
01:17:39.221 --> 01:17:42.180
And it is cool to see culture catching up to you.

1790
01:17:42.180 --> 01:17:45.221
And it's an honor and a pleasure to talk to you

1791
01:17:45.221 --> 01:17:46.117
(indistinct)
Thank you for having me.

1792
01:17:46.117 --> 01:17:49.140
If people wanna connect with you?

1793
01:17:49.140 --> 01:17:53.863
Well, my website is dr.weil.com, D-R-W-E-I-L.com.

1794
01:17:55.040 --> 01:17:58.363
The website of my center at the University of Arizona is

1795
01:17:58.363 --> 01:18:00.937
integrativemedicine.arizona.edu.

1796
01:18:00.937 --> 01:18:03.170
And that has a find a practitioner link

1797
01:18:03.170 --> 01:18:05.512
where you can find graduates of our program

1798
01:18:05.512 --> 01:18:09.308
who are in all states and all specialties.

1799
01:18:09.308 --> 01:18:10.813
Matcha.com is the website of Matcha Kari.

1800
01:18:14.132 --> 01:18:15.873
Rich 15.
Code, rich 15.

1801
01:18:15.873 --> 01:18:18.549
Use that and get your discount and--

1802
01:18:18.549 --> 01:18:20.040
And True Food Kitchen.

1803
01:18:20.040 --> 01:18:21.210
And True Food Kitchen.

1804
01:18:21.210 --> 01:18:22.457
I love True Food Kitchen.

1805
01:18:22.457 --> 01:18:25.387
Good. We've got 37 of them now.

1806
01:18:25.387 --> 01:18:28.640
And I think we'll open more and that's been very gratifying

1807
01:18:28.640 --> 01:18:30.980
to me because basically that's how I eat

1808
01:18:30.980 --> 01:18:33.420
and it's been good to see other people like--

1809
01:18:33.420 --> 01:18:35.545
Yeah, it's great, it's very tailored

1810
01:18:35.545 --> 01:18:36.378
to whenever you're--

1811
01:18:36.378 --> 01:18:37.259
And there is something for everyone.

1812
01:18:37.259 --> 01:18:39.150
I go in there as a vegan.

1813
01:18:39.150 --> 01:18:41.540
I never have any problems at all.

1814
01:18:41.540 --> 01:18:43.330
I've been to the one in Santa Monica

1815
01:18:43.330 --> 01:18:44.454
and I've been to ones when I'm on the road--

1816
01:18:44.454 --> 01:18:45.287
(words lost in cross talk)

1817
01:18:45.287 --> 01:18:46.120
